<p class=edition>Edition [EDITION]. This is a living document; the edition stamp identifies the vocabulary and fixture set that the Agent Skill [[AGENT-SKILLS]] and the conformance suite refer to.</p>

# Introduction # {#intro}

*This section is informative.*

A JAI document is an image compressed into words: a natural-language
description and, optionally, a few rendering parameters, from which a
generative model reconstructs the picture. The description is the code; the
model is the decoder. Two decoders produce different pixels, but the same
image in meaning and appearance, in the way two JPEG decoders produce slightly
different pixels from the same file, only much further along the same curve.

The simplest JAI document is a sentence:

<div class=example id=ex-intro-plain>
```
A magical castle
```
</div>

Parameters follow the description, separated by semicolons:

<div class=example id=ex-intro-params>
```
jai:A magical castle;style=anime;model=flux
```
</div>

The same document has a JSON form and a compressed file form, and all three
are the same document:

<div class=example id=ex-intro-forms>
Text form:
```
jai:A magical castle;model=flux;style=anime
```
JSON form:
```json
{"jai":1,"prompt":"A magical castle","model":"flux","style":"anime"}
```
File form: the JSON above as a single gzip member, in a file named `castle.jai`, served as `image/jai`, or embedded as `data:image/jai;base64,…`.
</div>

## Motivation ## {#intro-motivation}

*This section is informative.*

### A description is compression ### {#intro-compression}

Every image format trades bits for distortion. JPEG discards detail the eye
will not miss; JAI keeps only what a person would say about the picture and
discards the rest. In the terms of rate–distortion theory it sits at the far
low-rate end of the same curve: a typical document is 50 to 500 bytes for an
image of any resolution, against tens of kilobytes to megabytes for a raster,
and its distortion is measured in meaning and appearance (does the result show
the same things, arranged and lit the same way) rather than in pixel error.

This works because the decoder carries a prior. A generative model has
learned what harbours, castles and dusk light look like, so the code only has
to say which ones, where and how. Speech coding has relied on the same idea for
decades: a vocoder transmits the parameters of a model of the voice instead of
the waveform, and a CELP encoder finds those parameters by running the decoder
inside the encoder and keeping what sounds closest. JAI encoding is the same
*analysis by synthesis* ([[#image-encoding]]): an [=image encoder=] describes an image, renders the
description with the target models, compares the renders with the original,
and revises the description until they match as closely as time and budget
allow. A document can carry a core description shared by all models plus small
per-model refinements, so that each decoder in its preference list
reconstructs the image as well as it can.

The rate–distortion trade-off is the author's to choose: a sentence for a
decorative image, a few precise paragraphs for one whose look matters.

### Where it matters ### {#intro-uses}

JAI is designed for a world where generating an image is cheap and local, and
moving one is not.

- **Links with little bandwidth and enough compute.** Spacecraft and
    deep-space links, ships, aircraft, satellite connections with data caps,
    rural networks, disaster zones, and text-only channels such as SMS, packet
    radio and mesh networks can carry a sentence where they cannot carry a
    photograph. A receiver with a GPU reconstructs the picture.
- **Local generation.** Image generation is moving onto commodity GPUs and
    phones, at well under a second per image. When decoding is local and free,
    a browser can choose the JAI source in a `<picture>` element and never
    download the raster ([[#html-carriers]]); pages get lighter with no change
    to their markup.
- **An audience of one.** Because the reader's own decoder produces the
    pixels, the reader can ask for them differently: adapted to their culture,
    softened for sensitive content, adjusted for their eyesight, in the
    language of the text inside the image, or simply in a mood or style they
    enjoy, while the author's description stays the same
    ([[#viewer-personalisation]]).
- **Longevity and resolution independence.** A description does not age with
    its codec and has no fixed resolution. Rendered next year by a better
    model, or at a higher resolution, the same document yields a better
    picture, much as vector graphics outlive the screens they were drawn for.
- **Text all the way down.** A JAI image can be searched, translated,
    diffed in version control and edited with a text editor; it carries its
    own text alternative for screen readers and text-only browsers; and
    language models and agents can read and write images in the medium they
    already use.
- **Honest provenance.** A JAI image is generated by construction, so it
    cannot be mistaken for a photograph of something that happened.

Today, rendering usually runs on hosted providers through an API key. That is
the stage this edition is published in: good for demonstrations and for
personal renders, and the reason the security section spends so much care on
consent, budgets and what leaves the browser ([[#security]]). The format does
not depend on it.

## What JAI is and is not ## {#intro-scope}

*This section is informative.*

JAI describes visual intent, not pixels. It does not specify a neural network,
a sampler, a provider, or exact output. A decoder produces *an* image
consistent with the description, not *the* image; the encoder can only reduce
the distortion, never remove it. Decoding is non-deterministic across
decoders, versions, hardware and time. With hosted providers, as today, it may
also take seconds, cost money, and be refused by a provider's content policy,
and it has side effects that no other image format has: it transmits the
document, and possibly an input image, to a renderer, under the account of the
person viewing it.

Every JAI document also has a textual rendering that is instant, free and
always available: its description, the `prompt` values joined with line
breaks. A user agent that will not render pixels presents that instead.

Where exact pixels matter more than size, such as evidence, medical images,
diagrams and screenshots of text, ship the raster. JAI can still travel
alongside it as the editable, searchable source: HTML's `<picture>` element
carries both ([[#html-carriers]]).

## Audiences ## {#intro-audiences}

*This section is informative.*

This specification is written for six audiences: people and AI agents who
write JAI; authoring tools that convert images to JAI or validate it; library
developers implementing parsers and serialisers; extension developers adding
parameters or renderers; browser and extension developers implementing
discovery, rendering and replacement; and renderer developers turning a JAI
document into a picture. Sections [[#text]] through [[#file]] are the parts a
library developer needs; [[#parameters]] and [[#rendering]] are for renderer
developers; [[#html]] is for browser, extension and web developers; and
[[#authoring]] is a plain-language primer for authors.

# Conformance # {#conformance}

The key words "MUST", "MUST NOT", "REQUIRED", "SHALL", "SHALL NOT", "SHOULD",
"SHOULD NOT", "RECOMMENDED", "NOT RECOMMENDED", "MAY", and "OPTIONAL" in this
document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [[!RFC2119]]
[[!RFC8174]] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.
The same words in lower case have their ordinary English meaning.

Everything in this specification is normative except sections marked
informative, examples, notes, and the appendices so marked.

## Conformance classes ## {#conformance-classes}

This specification defines requirements for the following classes. An
implementation may belong to several.

: <dfn export>conforming document</dfn>
:: A JAI document in any of its forms that satisfies every requirement placed
    on documents and authors ([[#data-model]], [[#text]], [[#json]], [[#file]],
    [[#parameters]], [[#html]]).
: <dfn export>parser</dfn>
:: Converts the text form to a document ([[#text-parsing]]).
: <dfn export>JSON reader</dfn>
:: Converts the JSON form to a document ([[#json-reading]]).
: <dfn export>decoder</dfn>
:: Converts the bytes of a `.jai` file, an `image/jai` resource, or the body
    of a `data:image/jai` URL to a document ([[#decoding]]). A decoder contains
    a parser and a JSON reader.
: <dfn export>serialiser</dfn>
:: Produces the canonical text form and the canonical JSON form of a document
    ([[#text-serialising]], [[#json-canonical]]).
: <dfn export>encoder</dfn>
:: Produces the file form ([[#file-encoding]]). An encoder contains a
    serialiser.
: <dfn export>validator</dfn>
:: Reports advisory diagnostics about a document or its carrier
    ([[#diagnostics]]).
: <dfn export>interpreter</dfn>
:: Derives the interpreted parameters of a document from its values
    ([[#parameters-interpretation]]). Every renderer and every viewer contains
    an interpreter.
: <dfn export>renderer</dfn>
:: Produces an image from a document ([[#rendering]]).
: <dfn export>viewer</dfn>
:: Software acting for a user that discovers JAI documents in content, decides
    whether and when to render them, and presents the result: a browser, a
    browser extension, a chat client, an image viewer ([[#html]],
    [[#viewer-requirements]], [[#security]]).
: <dfn export>authoring tool</dfn>
:: Software that produces or edits JAI documents on behalf of an author.

## Determinism ## {#conformance-determinism}

Parsing, reading and decoding are *total* and *deterministic*: for every
input, including malformed input, a conforming implementation produces a
document (possibly empty) and a set of diagnostics, and two conforming
implementations produce the same document and the same parser-level
diagnostics for the same input. Error recovery is therefore specified, not
left to implementations. Nothing in this specification may throw, abort or
produce a partial result on malformed input. The guarantee covers inputs
within the minimum bounds of [[#security-bounds]]; beyond them, an
implementation that applies its bound yields the empty document with the
diagnostic named there.

## Conformance test suite ## {#conformance-tests}

The repository at <a href="https://github.com/gnistai/jai">github.com/gnistai/jai</a>
holds machine-readable fixtures under `tests/` ([[#fixtures]]). The fixtures
are normative: where the fixtures and the prose of this specification
disagree, the fixtures are correct and the prose has a bug. An implementation
may claim conformance for a class when it passes every fixture of that class
("JAI [EDITION] conforming parser").

<table class=fixture-map>
<thead><tr><th>Conformance class<th>Fixture classes it must pass
<tbody>
<tr><td>parser<td>`parse` (documents and diagnostics)
<tr><td>JSON reader<td>`json`
<tr><td>decoder<td>`parse`, `json`, `decode`, and the `url` cases of kind `data`
<tr><td>serialiser<td>`serialize` (`text`, `bare`, `json`, `identity`), and `roundtrip` except the file form
<tr><td>encoder<td>as serialiser, plus `serialize` `url` and the file form of `roundtrip`
<tr><td>interpreter<td>`interpret`
<tr><td>viewer<td>`html`, `url` (all kinds), and everything required of a decoder and an interpreter
<tr><td>validator<td>the `advisories` and `no_advisories` of every case
</table>

Renderers, authoring tools and conforming documents have no fixtures in this
edition.

# Terminology # {#terminology}

: <dfn export>document</dfn>
:: An instance of the data model ([[#data-model]]): a map from keys to lists of
    values, describing one image.
: <dfn export>key</dfn>
:: The name of a parameter: an ASCII string matching the [=key grammar=].
: <dfn export>value</dfn>
:: One string assigned to a key. A key may have several values, in order.
: <dfn export>parameter</dfn>
:: A key together with its values.
: <dfn export>standard key</dfn>
:: One of the eleven keys defined in [[#parameters]].
: <dfn export>prompt</dfn>
:: The values of the key `prompt`: the description of the image.
: <dfn export>description</dfn>
:: The textual rendering of a document: its prompt values joined with U+000A
    LINE FEED, in order.
: <dfn export>text form</dfn>
:: The human-readable serialisation of a document ([[#text]]):
    `jai:A magical castle;style=anime`.
: <dfn export>JSON form</dfn>
:: The JSON serialisation of a document ([[#json]]).
: <dfn export>file form</dfn>
:: The gzip-compressed JSON form ([[#file]]); the content of a `.jai` file.
: <dfn export>canonical form</dfn>
:: The unique text form or JSON form a serialiser produces for a document
    ([[#text-serialising]], [[#json-canonical]]).
: <dfn export>identity</dfn>
:: The SHA-256 digest of a document's canonical JSON form ([[#document-identity]]).
: <dfn export>JAI whitespace</dfn>
:: Exactly the 25 code points that have the Unicode `White_Space` property:
    U+0009 TAB, U+000A LF, U+000B VT, U+000C FF, U+000D CR, U+0020 SPACE,
    U+0085 NEL, U+00A0 NO-BREAK SPACE, U+1680 OGHAM SPACE MARK, U+2000 to
    U+200A inclusive, U+2028 LINE SEPARATOR, U+2029 PARAGRAPH SEPARATOR,
    U+202F NARROW NO-BREAK SPACE, U+205F MEDIUM MATHEMATICAL SPACE and
    U+3000 IDEOGRAPHIC SPACE. No other code point is whitespace for the
    purposes of this specification. U+FEFF is not whitespace.
: <dfn export>trim</dfn>
:: To remove every leading and trailing [=JAI whitespace=] code point from a
    string.

    <div class=note>
    `String.prototype.trim` (JavaScript), `str.strip()` (Python),
    `String.trim()` (Java) and the regular-expression class `\s` each remove a
    different set of code points, and none of them removes exactly this set;
    Go's `strings.TrimSpace` happens to match. Implementations use the list.
    </div>
: <dfn export>ASCII lowercase</dfn>
:: The string with every code point in the range U+0041 (A) to U+005A (Z)
    replaced by the code point 32 greater, and no other change [[!INFRA]].
: <dfn export>ASCII case-insensitive</dfn>
:: Two strings match ASCII case-insensitively when their [=ASCII lowercase=]
    forms are identical [[!INFRA]]. Implementations MUST NOT use
    locale-sensitive case mapping or Unicode case folding anywhere in this
    specification: under those, U+0130 (İ), U+0131 (ı) and U+212A (KELVIN
    SIGN) would match ASCII letters.
: <dfn export>scalar value string</dfn>
:: A string of Unicode scalar values, that is, a string containing no
    surrogate code points [[!INFRA]].
: <dfn export>author</dfn>
:: Whoever wrote a document.
: <dfn export>publisher</dfn>
:: The site or channel that delivers a document to a user. On a site that
    accepts user content the author and the publisher are different parties.
: <dfn export>user</dfn>
:: The person on whose behalf a viewer renders a document, and under whose
    renderer account the pixels are produced.
: <dfn export>original image</dfn>
:: Pixels that accompany a document in its carrier: the `<img>` element's own
    image in HTML, or the image named by the `source` key ([[#key-source]]).
: <dfn export>carrier</dfn>
:: A place in a host format where a document is found ([[#html-carriers]]).
: <dfn export>JAI context</dfn>
:: The content of a `.jai` file, of a resource served as `image/jai` or
    referenced from a [=carrier=], or the body of a `data:image/jai` URL.
    Attribute carriers are not JAI contexts: they always hold the text form.
: <dfn export>provider</dfn>
:: A service that runs a model for a renderer.
: <dfn export>layout box</dfn>
:: The box HTML lays out for an element, from its attributes and CSS.
: <dfn export>advisory</dfn>
:: Of a parameter: a preference the author expresses, which a renderer or
    viewer honours when it can and ignores, with a diagnostic, when it cannot;
    an advisory value never selects anything the user has not configured and
    never overrides a user setting.

# Data model # {#data-model}

A <a>document</a> is an ordered map from [=keys=] to lists of one or more
[=values=]. The order of values within a key is significant; the relative
order of distinct keys is not significant and carries no meaning.

A document describes exactly one image.

## Keys ## {#keys}

The <dfn export>key grammar</dfn> is, in ABNF [[!RFC5234]]:

```abnf
key      = name [ "/" target ]
name     = segment *( "." segment )
target   = segment
segment  = ALPHANUM *( ALPHANUM / "_" / "-" )
ALPHANUM = %x61-7A / DIGIT          ; a-z, 0-9
```

A key is a *name*, a non-empty sequence of segments separated by single full
stops, optionally followed by `/` and a *target*, one segment. Each segment
starts with a lowercase ASCII letter or digit and continues with lowercase
ASCII letters, digits, `_` and `-`. A key with a target, such as
`prompt/flux`, is a <dfn export>qualified key</dfn>: it holds refinements for
the models that target names ([[#model-refinements]]). Only the names
`prompt`, `style` and `avoid` take a target in this edition; parsers and
serialisers keep other qualified keys unchanged, and validators report them
with `invalid-qualifier`.

Keys are compared for equality after [=ASCII lowercase=] conversion: readers
convert every key they receive to ASCII lowercase before anything else, so a
document never contains two keys that differ only in case.

Keys without a `.` are reserved for this specification ([[#extensibility]]).

The key `jai` is reserved and is never a parameter: in the [=JSON form=] the
member named `jai` identifies the object as a JAI document ([[#json-marker]]);
in the [=text form=] an assignment to `jai` is dropped ([[#text-parsing]]).

<div class=note>
Implementations that use language objects as maps need real maps or
null-prototype objects: `constructor` is a valid key, and `__proto__`, while
not a valid key, is a valid JSON member name.
</div>

## Values ## {#values}

A [=value=] is a non-empty [=scalar value string=] that does not begin or end
with [=JAI whitespace=] and does not contain U+000D CARRIAGE RETURN or
U+0000 NULL.

Every reader of every form applies the following <dfn export>value normalisation</dfn>
to each candidate value before it enters the document, in this order:

1. Replace every surrogate code point with U+FFFD REPLACEMENT CHARACTER.
2. Replace every U+000D U+000A pair, and then every remaining U+000D, with
    U+000A.
3. Replace every U+0000 with U+FFFD. (Other control characters are kept as
    content; validators report them, see [[#diagnostics]]. Byte inputs never
    reach this step with U+0000: [[#decoding]] rejects the byte 00.)
4. [=Trim=] the string.
5. If the result is empty, discard the candidate and emit the diagnostic
    `empty-value`; otherwise the result is the value.

Values are strings and only strings. Numbers, booleans and structured data
exist only as their spelling (`strength=0.6`, `animate=true`); this is what
makes the text form able to represent every document.

No Unicode normalisation is applied anywhere in this specification. Two
strings are the same value if and only if they consist of the same sequence
of code points; canonically equivalent texts (for example NFC and NFD
spellings of the same word, or U+037E GREEK QUESTION MARK and `;`) are
different values and yield different [=identities=]. Parsers MUST NOT
normalise their input.

## Standard keys and repetition ## {#data-model-arity}

A document may assign any number of values to any key; the data model does not
know which keys are single-valued. The standard keys `aspect`, `mode`,
`strength`, `seed`, `source`, `preview` and `animate` are *single-valued at
interpretation*: an interpreter uses the last value and reports
`repeated-single` ([[#parameters-interpretation]]). Parsers and serialisers
keep every value; identity and round-tripping depend on all of them.

# Text form # {#text}

The text form is a string. It is used wherever a string fits: HTML
attributes, the body of a `data:image/jai` URL, chat messages, clipboards and
documentation.

## Grammar ## {#text-grammar}

*This grammar is informative; the algorithm in [[#text-parsing]] is
normative.*

```abnf
jai-text   = OWS [ prefix ] [ text-segment *( ";" text-segment ) ] [ ";" ]
prefix     = %s"jai:" OWS                  ; "jai:" is ASCII case-insensitive
text-segment = assignment / prose
assignment = OWS key-ci OWS "=" value   ; key-ci: the key grammar, ASCII case-insensitive
prose      = value
value      = *( escape / vchar )
escape     = "\" ( ";" / "=" / "\" )
vchar      = <any scalar value except ";" and "\"> / "\" <not followed by ";", "=" or "\">
OWS        = *( <JAI whitespace> )
```

A segment is an assignment if it matches `assignment`; otherwise it is prose.
A `\` that does not begin an `escape` is an ordinary character.

## Structure ## {#text-structure}

- The optional prefix `jai:` marks a string as JAI. It is required where the
    context does not already establish JAI (an `alt` attribute, a chat
    message); it is optional where the context does (a `data-jai` attribute,
    the body of a `data:image/jai` URL, a `.jai` file).
- Segments are separated by unescaped `;`. A segment whose text before its
    first unescaped `=` is, after [=trimming=], a [=key=] is an <dfn export>assignment</dfn>
    of the rest of the segment to that key. Every other segment is
    <dfn export>prose</dfn>.
- The first prose segment is the prompt. Any later prose segment is a
    <dfn export>continuation</dfn>: it belongs to the most recently assigned value,
    with the `;` that preceded it restored. This is what lets ordinary
    semicolons appear in descriptions without escaping.
- Empty segments and segments consisting only of [=JAI whitespace=] are
    ignored, so a trailing `;` and a doubled `;;` are harmless.
- Three escape sequences exist: `\;` is a literal semicolon, `\=` a literal
    equals sign, `\\` a literal backslash. A backslash before any other
    character is itself literal, so `C:\Users` needs no escaping.
- Keys are ASCII case-insensitive; `Style=anime` and `style=anime` are the
    same assignment. Values are never case-converted.
- Whitespace around keys, around `=` and at the ends of values is ignored;
    whitespace inside values is preserved.

<div class=example id=ex-text-structure>
<table class=data>
<thead><tr><th>Input<th>Document
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>A magical castle</code><td>prompt = <code>A magical castle</code>
<tr><td><code>jai:A magical castle;style=anime;model=flux;model=sdxl</code><td>prompt = <code>A magical castle</code>; style = <code>anime</code>; model = <code>flux</code>, <code>sdxl</code>
<tr><td><code>prompt=A magical castle</code><td>prompt = <code>A magical castle</code>
<tr><td><code>model=flux;prompt=A magical castle</code><td>model = <code>flux</code>; prompt = <code>A magical castle</code>
<tr><td><code>A wizard stands in a doorway; behind him, a dragon sleeps;model=flux</code><td>prompt = <code>A wizard stands in a doorway; behind him, a dragon sleeps</code>; model = <code>flux</code>
<tr><td><code>A castle ; style = anime ;</code><td>prompt = <code>A castle</code>; style = <code>anime</code>
<tr><td><code>source=data:image/webp;base64,UklGRkI=;style=anime</code><td>source = <code>data:image/webp;base64,UklGRkI=</code>; style = <code>anime</code> (<code>base64,…</code> is not an assignment)
<tr><td><code>; my key=value</code><td>prompt = <code>my key=value</code> (<code>my key</code> contains a space and is not a key; the leading <code>;</code> yields <code>empty-segment</code>)
<tr><td><code>http://example.org/?a=b</code><td>prompt = <code>http://example.org/?a=b</code> (<code>:</code> and <code>/</code> are not key characters)
<tr><td><code>Draw a graph\; y=2x</code><td>prompt = <code>Draw a graph; y=2x</code>
<tr><td><code>E\=mc2 explained</code><td>prompt = <code>E=mc2 explained</code>
</table>
</div>

### Sharp edges ### {#text-sharp-edges}

*This section is informative.*

The assignment rule is deliberately simple, and five consequences are worth
knowing. Validators report each of them ([[#diagnostics]]).

1. A description that *begins* with a word, an equals sign and more text is an
    assignment: `E=mc2 explained` assigns `mc2 explained` to the key `e`.
    Write `prompt=E=mc2 explained` or `E\=mc2 explained`.
2. Prose after an assignment continues that assignment, not the prompt:
    `style=anime;A castle` has no prompt and the style `anime;A castle`.
    Write the description first, or introduce it with `prompt=`. A new line
    does not start a parameter either; one-parameter-per-line layouts need
    `;` at the line ends.
3. Whitespace is allowed around `=`, so `A chalkboard; e = mc2 written on it`
    assigns to `e`. Write `e \= mc2`.
4. A `data:` URL whose media type carries parameters (`;charset=utf-8`,
    `;name=a.png`) contains `;key=` and is split there. Escape those
    semicolons (`data:image/svg+xml\;charset=utf-8,…`); the canonical
    serialiser does so. A plain `data:…;base64,` URL needs nothing, because
    `base64,…` is not an assignment.
5. A word, `/`, a word and `=` after a semicolon is a [=qualified key=]:
    `A ratio; a/b=c` assigns `c` to `a/b`. Write `a/b\=c` to keep it as text.

## Escaping arbitrary text ## {#text-escaping}

An authoring tool or template that places text it did not write into a
document MUST protect it. The function <dfn export>escape-prose</dfn>(*s*) replaces
every `\` with `\\`, then every `;` with `\;` and every `=` with `\=`. For any
string *s* whose [=value normalisation=] is *s* itself, the segment
`prompt=escape-prose(s)` parses as exactly one prompt value equal to *s*, and
`key=escape-prose(s)` as one value of *key*.

The `prompt=` is part of the recipe: a bare first segment that begins with
`jai:` would lose those characters to prefix removal.

<div class=note>
This escaping is in addition to the host format's own: HTML attribute
quoting, JSON string escaping, percent-encoding in URLs. Leading and trailing
whitespace and carriage returns in *s* are not preserved, because values never
contain them.
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-escape-prose>
A template inserting a product description:
```
data-jai="prompt=Red mug\; model\=XZ-200\; colour\=red;style=product photo"
```
parses to prompt = `Red mug; model=XZ-200; colour=red` and style =
`product photo`. Without escaping, `model` and `colour` would have become
parameters and the prompt would have been cut short.
</div>

## Parsing ## {#text-parsing}

The <dfn export>text parsing algorithm</dfn> takes a string *input* and returns a
document and a list of diagnostics. Transport decoding (percent-decoding,
HTML attribute decoding) has already happened ([[#html-transport]]); the
algorithm never decodes anything.

1. Let *diagnostics* be an empty list and *doc* an empty document.
2. Replace every surrogate code point in *input* with U+FFFD.
3. While *input* begins with U+FEFF, remove it.
4. Replace every U+000D U+000A pair in *input*, then every remaining U+000D,
    with U+000A.
5. Skip leading [=JAI whitespace=]: let *i* be the index of the first code
    point of *input* that is not JAI whitespace, or the length of *input*.
6. If *input* from *i* begins with the four code points `jai:` compared
    [=ASCII case-insensitively=], advance *i* past them.
7. Split *input* from *i* into <dfn export>raw segments</dfn>: scan left to right;
    a `\` followed by any code point consumes both code points as part of the
    current raw segment; an unescaped `;` ends the current raw segment and
    starts a new one; the end of the input ends the last raw segment. Raw
    segments keep their escape sequences and their whitespace.
8. Let *pending* be an empty list of (key, raw text) pairs and let *current*
    be null.
9. For each raw segment *raw*, in order:
    1. If *raw* is empty or consists only of JAI whitespace: unless *raw* is
        the last raw segment, append `empty-segment` to *diagnostics*; in
        either case continue with the next raw segment. (A trailing delimiter
        is allowed and not diagnosed; an interior empty segment is ignored
        with a diagnostic.)
    2. Let *eq* be the index of the first `=` in *raw* that is not preceded by
        a `\` that is part of an escape sequence, determined by the same
        left-to-right scan as step 7.
    3. If *eq* exists and the [=trimmed=] text of *raw* before *eq* matches the
        [=key grammar=] after [=ASCII lowercase=] conversion:
        1. Let *key* be that ASCII-lowercased text and *rest* the text of
            *raw* after *eq*.
        2. If *key* is `jai`: append `reserved-key` to *diagnostics*; set
            *current* to a discarded pair that is not in *pending*; continue.
        3. Append the pair (*key*, *rest*) to *pending* and set *current* to it.
    4. Otherwise, if *current* is null: append the pair (`prompt`, *raw*) to
        *pending* and set *current* to it.
    5. Otherwise: append `;` followed by *raw* to the raw text of *current*.
        (If *current* is the discarded pair, the text is dropped with it.)
10. For each pair (*key*, *text*) in *pending*, in order:
    1. Let *unescaped* be *text* with every escape sequence `\;`, `\=`, `\\`
        replaced by its second character, scanning left to right so that
        `\\;` (two backslashes, then a semicolon) yields `\` followed
        by an unescaped `;`.
    2. Apply [=value normalisation=] to *unescaped*. If it yields a value,
        append that value to the list for *key* in *doc*; otherwise the
        diagnostic `empty-value` has been emitted.
11. Return *doc* and *diagnostics*.

Trimming happens once, in step 10, so interior spacing around restored
semicolons is preserved exactly as typed (`A wizard ; behind` stays
`A wizard ; behind`), except that an ignored empty or whitespace-only segment
disappears together with one delimiter (`A; ;b` becomes `A;b`; write
`A\; \;b` to keep it).

The result is deterministic for every input and the algorithm runs in time
linear in the length of the input.

<div class=example id=ex-parse-edge>
<table class=data>
<thead><tr><th>Input<th>Document<th>Diagnostics
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>jai:</code><td>empty<td>
<tr><td><code>;;</code><td>empty<td><code>empty-segment</code> ×2
<tr><td><code>A castle;style=</code><td>prompt = <code>A castle</code><td><code>empty-value</code>
<tr><td><code>k=;a</code><td>k = <code>;a</code> (the empty assignment was continued)<td>
<tr><td><code>jai:jai=1;prompt=x</code><td>prompt = <code>x</code><td><code>reserved-key</code>
<tr><td><code>jai:A castle;jai=2;more</code><td>prompt = <code>A castle</code> (<code>more</code> continued the dropped assignment)<td><code>reserved-key</code>
<tr><td><code>  jai:A castle</code><td>prompt = <code>A castle</code><td>
<tr><td><code>jai: jai:A castle</code><td>prompt = <code>jai:A castle</code> (the prefix is removed once)<td>
<tr><td><code>JAİ:A castle</code> (U+0130)<td>prompt = <code>JAİ:A castle</code> (not the prefix)<td>
<tr><td><code>a\=b</code><td>prompt = <code>a=b</code><td>
<tr><td><code>A; ;b</code><td>prompt = <code>A;b</code><td><code>empty-segment</code>
<tr><td><code>a\\;style=x</code><td>prompt = <code>a\</code>; style = <code>x</code><td>
<tr><td><code>a\;style=x</code><td>prompt = <code>a;style=x</code><td>
<tr><td><code>mode=transform;style=anime</code><td>mode = <code>transform</code>; style = <code>anime</code> (a document without a prompt is valid)<td>
</table>
</div>

## Canonical text serialisation ## {#text-serialising}

A [=serialiser=] takes a document whose keys and values satisfy [[#data-model]]
and produces one string. Serialisers MUST reject or normalise input that does
not satisfy the data model (keys not lowercase, values not normalised).

1. Let *out* be `jai:`.
2. Let *segments* be an empty list.
3. For each value *v* of the key `prompt`, in order: if it is the first prompt
    value and *v* does not match the <dfn export>assignment pattern</dfn> (optional
    JAI whitespace, a [=key=], optional JAI whitespace, `=`, matched ASCII
    case-insensitively at the start of *v*), append [=escape-value=](*v*) to
    *segments*; otherwise append `prompt=` followed by escape-value(*v*).
4. For each [=qualified key=] whose name is `prompt`, in ascending order of
    key by code point, for each of its values *v* in order: append the key,
    `=`, and escape-value(*v*) to *segments*.
5. For each remaining key, in ascending order of key by code point, for each
    of its values *v* in order: append the key, `=`, and escape-value(*v*) to
    *segments*.
6. Join *segments* with `;`, append the result to *out*, and return *out*.

<dfn export>escape-value</dfn>(*v*) is one left-to-right pass over *v* that inserts a
`\` before:

- a `\` that is immediately followed by `;`, `=` or `\`, or that is the last
    code point of *v* (the serialiser, or a later concatenation, will place a
    delimiter after it);
- a `;` that is the first code point of *v*, or that is followed by optional
    JAI whitespace and then either the end of *v*, another `;`, or a [=key=]
    followed by optional JAI whitespace and `=`.

Each decision looks at the original *v*, never at text already escaped. As an
ECMAScript regular expression, with `WS` standing for the [=JAI whitespace=]
class and `KEY` for the [=key grammar=] (case-insensitively):

```js
v.replace(/\\(?=[;=\\]|$)|^;|;(?=WS*(?:$|;|KEY WS*=))/gi, m => '\\' + m)
```

`WS` and `KEY` are placeholders for the class and the pattern to substitute.

Implementations that escape in two passes MUST escape backslashes first. `=`
is never escaped by the serialiser; the `prompt=` form protects the one place
where it matters.

This escaping is deterministic and regular; it escapes a superset of the
characters that would be significant and is not the shortest possible
encoding. The canonical form of ordinary prose stays readable:

<div class=example id=ex-canonical>
<table class=data>
<thead><tr><th>Document<th>Canonical text
<tbody>
<tr><td>prompt = <code>A wizard stands; behind him, a dragon</code>; model = <code>flux</code><td><code>jai:A wizard stands; behind him, a dragon;model=flux</code>
<tr><td>prompt = <code>E=mc2 explained</code><td><code>jai:prompt=E=mc2 explained</code>
<tr><td>prompt = <code>;-) a smiley</code><td><code>jai:\;-) a smiley</code>
<tr><td>prompt = <code>ends with backslash\</code>; style = <code>x</code><td><code>jai:ends with backslash\\;style=x</code>
<tr><td>prompt = <code>a;;b</code><td><code>jai:a\;;b</code>
<tr><td>prompt = <code>A list; item=1</code><td><code>jai:A list\; item=1</code>
<tr><td>prompt = <code>C:\Users\me</code><td><code>jai:C:\Users\me</code>
<tr><td>style = <code>anime</code>; prompt = <code>A castle</code>; model = <code>flux</code>, <code>sdxl</code><td><code>jai:A castle;model=flux;model=sdxl;style=anime</code>
</table>
</div>

Parsing the canonical text of a document yields that document, and
serialising the result yields the same text (the canonical form is a fixed
point). The conformance suite checks both properties.

### Bare serialisation ### {#text-bare}

The <dfn export>bare serialisation</dfn> is the canonical text without the `jai:`
prefix, for contexts that establish JAI and are not displayed as text (the
body of a `data:image/jai` URL, a `data-jai` attribute). It is produced by the
same algorithm with one more case in step 3: the first prompt value is also
written as `prompt=…` when it begins with `jai:` ([=ASCII case-insensitively=]),
with U+FEFF, or with `{`.

<div class=note>
The `{` rule keeps the body of a `data:image/jai` URL from being mistaken for
the JSON form by a [=decoder=] ([[#decoding]]).
</div>

## Bidirectional text ## {#text-bidi}

*This section is informative.*

The structure of the text form is left-to-right: keys and delimiters are
ASCII. A canonical string always starts with `jai:`, which pins the base
direction of the whole string under first-strong detection; an
Arabic or Hebrew prompt therefore displays correctly in a plain text field.
The bare form, used only in attributes and URL bodies, is not displayed as
text. Authoring tools that show a document should display the canonical
string in a left-to-right container and each value with `dir="auto"` or
`unicode-bidi: isolate`. Trailing punctuation of a right-to-left prompt may
display beside the following `;key=`; parsing is unaffected. Validators report
explicit bidirectional controls and invisible formatting characters inside
values ([[#diagnostics]]).

# JSON form # {#json}

The JSON form is a JSON object [[!RFC8259]].

<div class=example id=ex-json>
```json
{"jai":1,"prompt":"A magical castle","model":["flux","sdxl"],"style":"anime"}
```
</div>

## Shape ## {#json-shape}

- The object MUST contain a member named `jai` whose value is the JSON number
    `1` ([[#json-marker]]).
- Every other member maps a [=key=] to either a JSON string (one value) or a
    JSON array of JSON strings (one or more values, in order). A string is
    equivalent to a one-element array.
- Member names MUST be valid keys (lowercase). A conforming document contains
    no two members whose names are equal after ASCII lowercasing.
- Member values are subject to [=value normalisation=] when read, so a
    conforming document contains no empty strings, no empty arrays and no
    strings that begin or end with JAI whitespace.
- An empty array contributes no values and is not diagnosed by readers;
    validators MAY report it.
- A member whose value is an object, or an array containing anything other
    than strings, is reserved for future revisions of this specification
    ([[#extensibility]]). Conforming documents of this edition contain none.

## The `jai` marker ## {#json-marker}

The member `jai` identifies a JSON object as a JAI document and gives the
[=file form=] its decompressed signature: the bytes `{"jai":1` followed by `,` or
`}` ([[#json-canonical]]).
Its value is the JSON number 1 (so `1.0` is accepted, because it is the same
number).

This specification does not expect to assign another value to the marker:
revisions add keys and reserved member shapes, and never change the meaning of
existing ones. An incompatible future format would use a different member
name or media type.

Readers behave as follows:

- value 1: proceed;
- member absent: in a [=JAI context=], proceed with the diagnostic
    `missing-marker`; in any other context the reader returns the empty document
    with no diagnostics (the object is not a JAI document);
- any other integer (including 0 and negative integers): proceed best-effort
    with the diagnostic `unsupported-version`; a viewer MUST NOT render such a
    document automatically;
- any other value (`"1"`, `true`, `null`, `1.5`): proceed as for 1 with the
    diagnostic `invalid-marker`.

The text form carries no marker; its prefix plays that role.

## Reading ## {#json-reading}

The <dfn export>JSON reading algorithm</dfn> takes a JSON text and a flag *in JAI
context* and returns a document and diagnostics.

1. Parse the text as JSON [[!RFC8259]]. Texts that are not valid RFC 8259 JSON
    (including `NaN`, `Infinity`, comments and trailing commas), and texts
    containing a number whose value, rounded to the nearest IEEE 754 binary64
    value, is infinite (for example `1e400`) wherever it occurs, the `jai`
    member included, yield the empty document with the diagnostic
    `invalid-json`. If the top-level value is not an object, yield the empty
    document with `invalid-json`. If arrays and objects are nested more than 32
    deep anywhere in the text (the top-level object has depth 1), yield the
    empty document with `invalid-json`. Implementations whose JSON parser
    recurses check this with a scan before parsing, skipping the contents of
    strings, so that no input exhausts the stack.
2. Read the members into an insertion-ordered map keyed by the exact member
    name. A member name that repeats an earlier one replaces that member's
    value in place and keeps the earlier position (this is what ECMAScript
    `JSON.parse` and Python `json.loads` do). Readers do not diagnose an exact
    repetition; validators MAY report it as `duplicate-member`.
3. Handle the marker as in [[#json-marker]], then remove it from the map. If
    the marker is absent and *in JAI context* is false, return the empty
    document and no diagnostics.
4. For each remaining member, in map order:
    1. Let *name* be the [=ASCII lowercase=] of the member name. If *name*
        does not match the [=key grammar=], emit `invalid-key` and skip the
        member. If *name* is `jai`, emit `duplicate-member` and skip it.
    2. Let *items* be the member value if it is an array, otherwise a
        one-element list containing the value.
    3. For each item: a string is a candidate value; a number is converted to
        a candidate value by the ECMAScript `Number::toString` algorithm
        applied to its IEEE 754 binary64 value, as RFC 8785 §3.2.2.3 specifies
        ([[!RFC8785]]), with the diagnostic `number-value`; `true` and `false`
        become the candidates `true` and `false` with `number-value`; `null`
        is skipped with `empty-value`; an object or an array is skipped with
        `unsupported-member`.
    4. Apply [=value normalisation=] to each candidate and append the resulting
        values, in order, to the list for *name*. If a member earlier in the map has a different
        name that also lowercases to *name*, emit `duplicate-key` for *name*,
        once for this member, whether or not either member contributed values.
        The values of all such members are concatenated in map order.
5. Return the document and diagnostics.

<div class=note>
Number conversion follows ECMAScript so that a hand-written
`"strength": 0.6` is forgiven, and so that it is the same in every language:
`1.0` → `1`, `1E2` → `100`, `0.60` → `0.6`, `-0` → `0`, `1e21` → `1e+21`,
`5e-324` → `5e-324`. Integers beyond 2<sup>53</sup> lose precision in this
conversion; authors write such values as JSON strings ([[#key-seed]]
requires it). Python readers need `parse_constant` to reject `NaN` and `Infinity`
and a short emulation of `Number::toString`; the conformance suite pins the
cases.
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-json-reading>
<table class=data>
<thead><tr><th>JSON<th>Document<th>Diagnostics
<tbody>
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"prompt":" A castle "}</code><td>prompt = <code>A castle</code><td>
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"prompt":"a\r\nb"}</code><td>prompt = <code>a</code> U+000A <code>b</code><td>
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"Prompt":"a","prompt":"b"}</code><td>prompt = <code>a</code>, <code>b</code><td><code>duplicate-key</code>
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"strength":0.60,"animate":true}</code><td>strength = <code>0.6</code>; animate = <code>true</code><td><code>number-value</code> ×2
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"seed":12345678901234567890}</code><td>seed = <code>12345678901234567000</code><td><code>number-value</code> (precision lost)
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"prompt":["a",null,"",["b"],{"x":1}," c "]}</code><td>prompt = <code>a</code>, <code>c</code><td><code>empty-value</code> ×2, <code>unsupported-member</code> ×2
<tr><td><code>{"jai":"1","prompt":"x"}</code><td>prompt = <code>x</code><td><code>invalid-marker</code>
<tr><td><code>{"jai":2,"prompt":"x"}</code><td>prompt = <code>x</code><td><code>unsupported-version</code>
<tr><td><code>{"prompt":"x"}</code> in a <code>.jai</code> file<td>prompt = <code>x</code><td><code>missing-marker</code>
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"__proto__":"x","prompt":"x"}</code><td>prompt = <code>x</code><td><code>invalid-key</code>
</table>
</div>

## Canonical JSON ## {#json-canonical}

The <dfn export>canonical JSON form</dfn> of a document is the following byte
sequence, and every serialiser produces exactly it:

- UTF-8 without a byte order mark; no whitespace outside strings.
- Members in this order: `jai`, then `prompt` if present, then the
    [=qualified keys=] whose name is `prompt` in ascending code point order,
    then every other key in ascending code point order of the key.
- The value of `jai` is `1`. Every other member's value is a JSON string when
    the key has one value and a JSON array of strings when it has several.
- Strings are serialised exactly as RFC 8785 §3.2.2.2 [[!RFC8785]] specifies:
    `"` as `\"`, `\` as `\\`, U+0008, U+0009, U+000A, U+000C and U+000D as
    `\b`, `\t`, `\n`, `\f` and `\r`, every other code point below U+0020 as
    `\u` followed by four lowercase hexadecimal digits, and every other code
    point, including U+007F, U+2028, U+2029 and all non-ASCII, literally.

Because values are [=scalar value strings=], canonical JSON never contains
surrogate escapes.

<div class=note>
This is RFC 8785 (the JSON Canonicalization Scheme) with one deviation: the
members `jai` and `prompt` come first, so that every file begins with the
bytes `{"jai":1` followed by `,` or `}` after decompression and a reader can
see the description first. An implementation can run a JCS library and move those two members to
the front; the string and number rules are identical. In JavaScript the
canonical form is `JSON.stringify` of an object built in this member order;
in Python, `json.dumps(obj, ensure_ascii=False, separators=(',', ':'))` with
members inserted in order; Go callers need `SetEscapeHTML(false)`.
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-json-canonical>
Document: style = `anime`; prompt = `A castle`, `with a moat`; model = `flux`.
```json
{"jai":1,"prompt":["A castle","with a moat"],"model":"flux","style":"anime"}
```
</div>

## Identity ## {#document-identity}

The identity of a document is the SHA-256 digest of its canonical
JSON form, written as 64 lowercase hexadecimal digits.

Identity is syntactic: two documents are the same document if and only if
their canonical JSON forms are byte-identical. Documents that differ in any
member, including `preview`, vendor keys, or the spelling `16x9` versus
`16:9`, are different documents. Identity is the same whichever form or
carrier delivered the document, and whatever whitespace, key case or key
order the author used.

Identity names a document, not a render. A render cache key MUST include,
besides the identity, the renderer and its configuration, and the identity of
every image input the render used (an explicit `source` or the carrier's
[=original image=]). See also [[#security-cache]].

Identity is public information and MUST NOT be used as a capability or as a
key into an allow- or deny-list.

# File form, media type and decoding # {#file}

## File form ## {#file-format}

The [=file form=] of a document is exactly one gzip member [[!RFC1952]] with
compression method 8 (deflate), followed by nothing, whose decompressed
content is the [=canonical JSON form=]. A `.jai` file contains the file form.

Decoders MUST reject, as described in [[#decoding]]:

- a member whose reserved FLG bits are set;
- a CRC32 or ISIZE that does not match the decompressed content;
- any bytes after the end of the member, including a second member;
- decompressed output exceeding the decoder's bound ([[#security-bounds]]),
    which is enforced on the decompressed byte count during inflation and never
    read from ISIZE;
- decompressed content that is not a JSON object (including content that is
    itself gzip);
- a header with FLG.FHCRC set whose two header-CRC bytes are not the two least
    significant bytes of the CRC32 of all header bytes before them;
- a deflate stream [[!RFC1951]] that is invalid: a block of type 3; a stored
    block whose LEN is not the ones' complement of NLEN; more than 286
    literal/length codes or more than 30 distance codes (HLIT + 257 > 286 or
    HDIST + 1 > 30); a code-length code that is over-subscribed or incomplete; a
    literal/length or distance code that is over-subscribed, or that is
    incomplete unless it consists of exactly one code of length 1 (a distance
    code with no codes at all is accepted); a literal/length code with no code
    for symbol 256; a repeat code 16 with no previous length, or code lengths
    that run past the HLIT + 257 + HDIST + 1 lengths announced; a code in the
    compressed data that is assigned no symbol, a length symbol 286 or 287, or a
    distance symbol 30 or 31; a distance greater than the number of bytes output
    so far; or a stream that ends before its final block ends. These are exactly
    the streams zlib's inflate rejects, so `DecompressionStream`, Node's `zlib`
    and Python's `zlib` can be used unmodified.

The header fields FEXTRA, FNAME, FCOMMENT, MTIME, XFL and OS are read and
ignored; FNAME is never used as a file name. Byte equality of two `.jai`
files is not a conformance notion: the canonical form is defined at the JSON
level.

## Encoding ## {#file-encoding}

An [=encoder=] produces the file form from a document. Encoders MUST write
the canonical JSON form as the member content. Encoders SHOULD write MTIME as
0 and SHOULD NOT write FNAME or FCOMMENT, so that files carry no timestamps or
file names and tests are reproducible.

<div class=note>
`gzip -n` and the default settings of most library encoders already satisfy
this. A file produced by `gzip -c castle.json > castle.jai` from canonical
JSON is a conforming file whatever its header fields.
</div>

<div class=note>
Why gzip, when a forty-byte description becomes an eighty-byte file: gzip is
the one compression every browser (`DecompressionStream`), runtime, language
and CDN decodes natively; it undoes almost all of the base64 inflation of an
embedded `preview`; and it gives the format one encoding rule instead of
"compress when large". Contexts where the twenty extra bytes matter use the
text form in a `data:` URL instead ([[#data-urls]]).
</div>

## Media type and file extension ## {#media-type}

The media type of a JAI document in any of its three serialisations is
<dfn export>`image/jai`</dfn>. The file extension is `.jai`. The `type` attribute
value `image/jai` ([[#html-carriers]]) is matched by its essence, [=ASCII
case-insensitively=], ignoring parameters. A `charset` parameter has no
meaning: the text and JSON forms are always UTF-8.

<div class=note>
`image/jai` is used unregistered, as `image/webp` was for a decade before
RFC 9649. Appendix [[#iana]] carries the registration template; registration
in the standards tree requires an IETF or SDO process that the project intends
to pursue once the format is stable. Should the type be registered under a
different name, viewers will match both. JAI is unrelated to JPEG AI
(ISO/IEC 6048), whose registered types are `image/jaii` and `image/jais`.
</div>

Servers SHOULD send `Content-Type: image/jai` for `.jai` resources, and, as
for web fonts, SHOULD send `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` so that scripts on
other origins can read them; a `.jai` resource contains no user data. Servers
need not apply `Content-Encoding: gzip`; if they do, `fetch()` removes it and
the decoder sees JSON. Decoders apply [[#decoding]] to the bytes of any
resource referenced from a JAI carrier regardless of the served
`Content-Type`, because static hosts commonly serve unknown extensions as
`application/octet-stream`.

## Decoding ## {#decoding}

The <dfn export>decoding algorithm</dfn> takes a byte sequence *bytes* and a flag *in
JAI context* (true in a [=JAI context=]) and returns a document and
diagnostics. It never falls back from one serialisation to another; each
layer either succeeds or yields the empty document.

1. If *bytes* begins with `1F 8B`:
    1. Decode exactly one gzip member under the output bound. If the member is
        malformed in any way listed in [[#file-format]], if bytes follow it, or
        if the bound is exceeded, return the empty document with
        `malformed-container`, discarding any partial output.
    2. Let *bytes* be the decompressed content, and let *from gzip* be true.
2. If *from gzip* is false and *bytes* is longer than the decoder's text bound
    ([[#security-bounds]]), return the empty document with `undecodable`.
3. Decode *bytes* as UTF-8. If the bytes are not well-formed UTF-8 [[!ENCODING]]
    or contain the byte 00, return the empty document with
    `malformed-container` if *from gzip* is true and with `undecodable`
    otherwise. There is no autodetection of UTF-16 or other encodings and no
    `charset` handling. (The byte 00 never occurs in text a person or tool
    writes; rejecting it keeps binary data, such as UTF-16 without a byte order
    mark, from becoming a prompt.)
4. Remove every leading U+FEFF.
5. Let *i* be the index of the first code point that is not JSON whitespace
    (U+0009, U+000A, U+000D, U+0020). If the code point at *i* is `{` and the
    next code point after *i* that is not JSON whitespace is `"` or `}`, the
    input is in the JSON form: run the [=JSON reading algorithm=] on the
    text with *in JAI context*. A JSON failure is final: do not try the text
    form.
6. Otherwise, if *from gzip* is true, return the empty document with
    `malformed-container` (a gzip member must contain JSON).
7. Otherwise, the input is in the text form: run the [=text parsing
    algorithm=] on the text.

A `.jai` file whose bytes are not gzip is accepted by this algorithm but is
not a conforming file; validators report `non-canonical-encoding`.

<div class=note>
The three serialisations are distinguishable at the first non-whitespace
bytes: `1F 8B`, `{` followed by `"` or `}`, or anything else. A text
description that begins with `{` but is not followed by a quote, for example
`{masterpiece}, a castle`, is text. The [=bare serialisation=] writes a
`{`-initial prompt as `prompt=…` so that canonical output never meets the
JSON test.
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-decode>
<table class=data>
<thead><tr><th>Bytes<th>Result
<tbody>
<tr><td>gzip member containing <code>{"jai":1,"prompt":"A castle"}</code><td>prompt = <code>A castle</code>
<tr><td>the same followed by a second gzip member<td>empty, <code>malformed-container</code>
<tr><td>gzip member containing <code>jai:A castle</code><td>empty, <code>malformed-container</code>
<tr><td><code>{"jai":1,"prompt":"A castle"}</code> (uncompressed)<td>prompt = <code>A castle</code>; validator: <code>non-canonical-encoding</code>
<tr><td><code>{masterpiece}, a castle</code><td>prompt = <code>{masterpiece}, a castle</code>
<tr><td><code>{"quoted"} prose</code><td>empty, <code>invalid-json</code>
<tr><td><code>FF FE 6A 00 …</code> (UTF-16)<td>empty, <code>undecodable</code>
<tr><td>the bytes of a PNG file<td>empty, <code>undecodable</code>
<tr><td><code>jai:A castle</code><td>prompt = <code>A castle</code>
</table>
</div>

## `data:` URLs ## {#data-urls}

A document may be embedded in a `data:` URL [[!RFC2397]] with the media type
`image/jai`, in any of the three serialisations:

<div class=example id=ex-data-urls>
```
data:image/jai,A%20magical%20castle;style=anime
data:image/jai;base64,H4sIAAAAAAACA6tWykrMVLIy1FEqKMrPLShRslJyVMhNTM9MTsxRSE4sLslJVdJRKi6pBNJWSol5mbmpSrUAq6nXdjUAAAA=
```
Both URLs carry the same document.
</div>

The body of the URL, after the data: URL processor of the Fetch Standard has
percent-decoded or base64-decoded it [[!FETCH]], is decoded with
[[#decoding]] with *in JAI context* true (the body is a [=JAI context=]). The non-base64 text form is the
most compact and the only human-readable option; the base64 file form is
permitted and never required. Media-type parameters other than `base64` are
ignored.

If the data: URL processor returns failure (no `,`, or a `;base64` body that
forgiving-base64 rejects), or the essence of its MIME type is not `image/jai`,
the URL carries no document. The MIME type is parsed, as Fetch does, from the
serialised URL, in which the URL parser has percent-encoded non-ASCII and
control code points, so `data:image/jai` followed by U+00A0 is not
`image/jai`.

When a serialiser or encoder writes the text form or the JSON form into the body of a
`data:image/jai,` URL it MUST percent-encode every byte of the UTF-8 encoding
that is a C0 control, U+007F, an ASCII whitespace character (U+0020 as `%20`,
U+000A as `%0A`, U+0009 as `%09`), `%` (as `%25`), `#` (as `%23`), `"` (as `%22`), `'` (as `%27`), `<` (as `%3C`),
`>` (as `%3E`), and a `,`
that would be the last byte of the URL (as `%2C`); a body beginning with `/`
is written with `%2F`. Every other byte, including `;`, `=`, `,`, `{`, `}`, `?`, `/`,
`&`, `+` and non-ASCII, MAY remain literal. The
<dfn export>canonical URL form</dfn> of a document is `data:image/jai,` followed by
its [=bare serialisation=] so encoded, so that the canonical URL form can be
placed in any quoted HTML attribute without further escaping.

<div class=note>
Unencoded whitespace in a `srcset` attribute silently discards the candidate;
an unencoded `#` ends the URL, so everything after it is lost; a literal `%`
followed by two hexadecimal digits is decoded as a byte. Line breaks have to be
`%0A`: every URL parser deletes raw tabs and line feeds. Percent-decoding is
a transport step performed once by the URL processor; `%3B` and `%3D` become
`;` and `=` and then parse as delimiters, exactly as if typed. Only `\;` and
`\=` protect prose ([[#text-escaping]]).
</div>

# Standard parameters # {#parameters}

This edition defines eleven [=standard keys=]. Each has a meaning a renderer
can act on without knowing anything about a particular model. All of them are
[=advisory=] in the sense of [[#terminology]]: a renderer honours what it can
and reports what it cannot ([[#rendering-diagnostics]]); none of them can
select, fetch or configure anything the user has not configured.

<table class=keys>
<thead><tr><th>Key<th>Values<th>Form<th>Meaning
<tbody>
<tr><td>`prompt`<td>many<td>text<td>What the image shows. The [=description=] is the values joined with U+000A, in order.
<tr><td>`style`<td>many<td>text<td>Visual treatment: `anime`, `watercolor`, `35mm film photo`. All values apply.
<tr><td>`avoid`<td>many<td>text<td>Things or qualities the image should not contain, one per value, stated positively (`people`, not `no people`).
<tr><td>`model`<td>many<td>identifier<td>Ordered preference list of models or rendering packages. A selector, never a locator.
<tr><td>`aspect`<td>one<td>`W:H`, `WxH`, decimal, `square`, `landscape`, `portrait`<td>Preferred aspect ratio.
<tr><td>`mode`<td>one<td>`generate`, `transform`, `keep`<td>How the description relates to an [=original image=]. Default `generate`.
<tr><td>`strength`<td>one<td>decimal 0 to 1, or percentage<td>In `transform`, how far the result may depart from the source: 0 unchanged, 1 unconstrained.
<tr><td>`seed`<td>one<td>integer<td>Reproducibility hint, passed through when the model accepts one.
<tr><td>`source`<td>one<td>URL or `data:` URL of a raster image<td>The image to transform. Takes precedence over the carrier's own image.
<tr><td>`preview`<td>one<td>`data:` URL of a tiny raster image<td>Placeholder shown while rendering. Never an input to generation.
<tr><td>`animate`<td>one<td>boolean<td>Requests a looping animated image where supported; otherwise a still frame.
</table>

## Interpretation ## {#parameters-interpretation}

An [=interpreter=] derives the <dfn export>interpreted parameters</dfn> of a
document from its values. Interpretation never modifies the document: the
serialiser writes values as the author wrote them, and `16x9` and `16:9`
remain different documents with the same interpretation.

For each single-valued standard key (`aspect`, `mode`, `strength`, `seed`,
`source`, `preview`, `animate`) that is present:

1. If the key has several values, let *v* be the last value and emit
    `repeated-single` for the key; otherwise let *v* be its value. Appending a
    parameter to a shared default string is how an author overrides a
    single-valued key. This does not work for `prompt`, `style`, `avoid` and
    `model`, whose values accumulate; this edition has no way to clear an
    inherited value (`;style=` is dropped with `empty-value`), so keep those
    keys out of shared prefixes.
2. If *v* begins and ends with the same ASCII quotation mark (`"` or `'`) and
    is at least two code points long, remove both and [=trim=] the result
    (validators report `quoted-value`). Then, if *v* ends with `.`, remove it.
3. Match *v* against the key's value grammar below, [=ASCII
    case-insensitively=] where keywords are involved. On failure emit
    `invalid-value` for the key (for `source`, `invalid-image`; for `preview`,
    `unsupported-preview`), treat the key as absent, and, where the grammar
    suggests one, offer the corrected spelling in the diagnostic.

The values of `prompt`, `style`, `avoid` and `model` are used exactly as they
appear in the document: no quotation mark or full stop is removed, and
[[#model-matching]] receives the document's values.

Interpreters MUST NOT use lenient or locale-aware number parsers; the
grammars below are ASCII and exact. Non-ASCII digits, full-width characters
and the decimal comma are invalid (an authoring tool offers `0,6` → `0.6`,
`１６：９` → `16:9`).

### `prompt`, `style`, `avoid` ### {#key-text}

Any value is valid. The interpreted prompt is the [=description=]: the values
joined with a single U+000A in order. `prompt=a;prompt=b` renders as the same
input as a single value `a` U+000A `b` but is a different document. Renderers
MAY normalise whitespace for their model.

`style` values are combined; all apply. `avoid` values are independent
items; `avoid=text;avoid=people` and `avoid=text, people` reach a negative
prompt identically but are different documents. Validators report an `avoid`
value that begins with `no`, `not`, `without` or `never` followed by
whitespace (`negated-avoid`), because negation inside a negative prompt
inverts the author's intent on most models.

### `model` ### {#key-model}

Any value is valid. Values are matched against the renderers the user has
configured as described in [[#model-matching]]. A value promises nothing: the
same `model` value may select different models on different days or at
different providers, and a value the user has not configured selects
nothing.

Values are opaque identifiers. Validators MAY warn when a value looks like a
URL or a file path (`model-looks-like-locator`), because such values are
never fetched ([[#security-selectors]]).

### `aspect` ### {#key-aspect}

```abnf
aspect  = ratio / number / "square" / "landscape" / "portrait"
ratio   = number OWS ( ":" / "x" / %x00D7 / "/" ) OWS number   ; ABNF strings are case-insensitive
number  = 1*DIGIT [ "." *DIGIT ] / "." 1*DIGIT
```

A ratio denotes width divided by height; `1920x1080` and `16:9` denote the
same ratio, 16/9. A bare number denotes the ratio itself (`1.5`). Both
numbers of a ratio MUST be greater than zero. A ratio whose quotient W/H, or a
bare number, is greater than 100 or less than 0.01 is invalid. `square` is 1:1. `landscape` and
`portrait` express intent (wider than tall, taller than wide) without a
number; renderers SHOULD treat them as 4:3 and 3:4 unless they have a
different default for the model. `W:H` with integers is the canonical
spelling for authoring tools to suggest.

Renderers use the nearest ratio they support and report `adjusted-value`
when the result differs materially from the request. The relationship between
`aspect` and an original image is defined in [[#rendering-size]].

### `mode` ### {#key-mode}

One of `generate`, `transform`, `keep`, matched ASCII case-insensitively.
Semantics in [[#rendering-modes]]. Absent or invalid: `generate`.

### `strength` ### {#key-strength}

```abnf
strength = number [ OWS "%" ]
```

A number from 0 to 1 inclusive, or a percentage from 0% to 100% which is
divided by 100 (`60%` is 0.6). A value outside the range is clamped to it
with `adjusted-value`. 0 means the source is unchanged, 1 means the source
constrains nothing. Renderers map this linearly onto the model's own control;
a control that measures image influence rather than departure takes
1 − strength. `strength` has an effect only in `transform` mode; validators
report `strength-without-transform` otherwise.

### `seed` ### {#key-seed}

One to twenty ASCII digits. Renderers pass the value through when it lies
within the model's accepted range and otherwise reduce it deterministically
(for example modulo the range) with `adjusted-value`. A seed promises
nothing across renderers, model versions or hardware; a renderer that cannot
honour it reports `unsupported-parameter`, and one that runs a
non-deterministic mode reports `seed-nondeterministic`. Authors MUST write
seeds above 2<sup>53</sup> as strings in the JSON form ([[#json-reading]]).

### `source` ### {#key-source}

A URL, absolute or relative to the carrier's base URL, or a `data:` URL,
resolving to a raster image of type `image/png`, `image/jpeg`, `image/webp`,
`image/gif` or `image/avif`. Other types, including SVG, HTML and
`image/jai`, are invalid (`invalid-image`): a document never contains another
document as an input.

An interpreter checks a `source` value without fetching it. A `data:` value
is invalid when the data: URL processor returns failure or the essence of its
MIME type is not one of the types above. Any other value that begins with a URL
scheme (an ASCII letter followed by ASCII letters, digits, `+`, `-` or `.`,
then `:`) is invalid unless the scheme is `http` or `https`, ASCII
case-insensitively. An invalid `source` is reported with `invalid-image` and
treated as absent, so it never also yields `ignored-value`. A valid value's
type is known only when it is fetched; one that cannot be fetched, or does not
decode as one of these types, is not readable ([[#security-inputs]]).

A valid `source` is used only in `transform` mode (otherwise `ignored-value`). When
present it takes precedence over the carrier's [=original image=]. A `source`
URL is fetched only with the privileges of the document that carries it, and
only after the consent that the render itself requires ([[#security-inputs]]).
Documents intended for text channels SHOULD reference a URL rather than embed
bytes.

### `preview` ### {#key-preview}

A `data:` URL of a raster image of one of the types listed for `source`,
RECOMMENDED at most 64 pixels on the long side and at most 1 KiB. A viewer
shows it, scaled to the layout box, while a render is pending or when no
original image exists. It is never an input to generation and never fetched
from a network. Other values are ignored with `unsupported-preview`.

<div class=note>
A 4×4 to 8×8 pixel lossy WebP is about 80 to 130 bytes, or 110 to 180
characters of `data:` URL, and shows the image's colour layout. Hash-based
placeholders such as ThumbHash [[THUMBHASH]] and BlurHash [[BLURHASH]] are
three to four times smaller but need decoder code; a later edition might admit
them as prefixed values without affecting this edition's viewers.
</div>

### `animate` ### {#key-animate}

`true`, `yes`, `1`, `on` (true) or `false`, `no`, `0`, `off` (false), matched
ASCII case-insensitively. True requests a looping animated image resource
(GIF, APNG, animated WebP or animated AVIF) of renderer-chosen length that is
displayable wherever a still image is; video containers are not JAI render
results. A renderer that cannot produce one renders a still frame and reports
`unsupported-parameter`. Viewer obligations for motion are in
[[#viewer-accessibility]].

## Model matching ## {#model-matching}

A renderer, or a viewer choosing among configured renderers, resolves the
`model` values as follows. Only models the user has configured and enabled take
part ([[#security-selectors]]).

1. For each `model` value *v* in document order:
    1. If *v* matches, [=ASCII case-insensitively=], the identifier or an alias
        of an eligible model, select that model.
    2. Otherwise, unless the renderer pins versions: if the last
        `/`-separated segment of *v* contains `:` or `@` after its first code
        point, let *v′* be *v* with the text from the last such `:` or `@` to
        the end removed. If *v′* matches as in step 1.1, select that model and
        report `version-ignored`.
    3. Otherwise, let the *words* of a string be its maximal runs of ASCII
        letters and digits, ASCII-lowercased. A known family *occurs* in a list
        of words when the words of its name, or of one of its spellings, appear
        there as a contiguous run, and it has at least one eligible model.
        Search the words of the last `/`-separated segment of *v*; if no family
        occurs there, search the words of the whole of *v*. If several families
        occur in the same search, choose the one whose run starts earliest; at
        the same start, the one with the longer run; then the one the renderer
        lists first. Select that family's default model and report
        `substituted-model`.
2. If no value selected a model, including when the document has no `model`
    value, use the user's default model and report `no-matching-model`.
3. A model is *eligible* if it is configured and enabled and, in `transform`
    mode with an input image, accepts an image input. Step 2 uses the user's
    default model even when it is not eligible; in `transform` mode with an
    input image it is then rendered as `generate` with `mode-degraded`
    ([[#rendering-modes]]).

The *known families* are the families the renderer lists, in its order,
followed by the `family` of every configured model not already listed, in
configuration order; a family's name is always one of its spellings. The
*default model of a family* is the user's default model if it is eligible and
belongs to that family, and otherwise the first eligible configured model of
that family.

<div class=example id=ex-model-matching>
With families `flux` (spellings `flux`) and `sdxl` (`sdxl`, `stable diffusion
xl`): `black-forest-labs/FLUX.1-dev` → the words of the last segment are
`flux`, `1`, `dev` → family flux. `stable-diffusion-xl-base-1.0` → `stable`,
`diffusion`, `xl`, … → family sdxl. `https://example.org/flux-schnell` → last
segment `flux-schnell` → flux (and a validator warns that the value looks like
a URL). `stability-ai/sdxl:7762fd07` → no exact match; without the version
`:7762fd07` it is the exact id `stability-ai/sdxl` (`version-ignored`).
`replicate:stability-ai/sdxl:7762fd07` → no exact match with or without the
version; the words of the last segment, `sdxl` and `7762fd07`, give family
sdxl (`substituted-model`). `replicate:flux` → the segment `replicate:flux`
without `:flux` matches nothing, so nothing is removed; the words `replicate`,
`flux` give family flux.
</div>

Appendix [[#families]] lists family names informatively. There is no
registry of models: a renderer advertises what it knows, and the user
configures what the renderer may use.

## Per-model refinements ## {#model-refinements}

A document can carry a core description that every model receives, plus
refinements for particular models in [=qualified keys=]:

<div class=example id=ex-refinements>
```
jai:A girl holding a red balloon in a park at dusk;prompt/flux=The balloon floats high above her;prompt/imagen=The balloon is glossy;model=flux;model=imagen
```
</div>

The <dfn>selected model</dfn> is the model that [[#model-matching]] selects,
or the model the user chose when a viewer lets the user override the
document's preference. Once a model is selected, an interpreter forms the
values it uses for `prompt`, `style` and `avoid`: the core values of the key,
followed by the values of every qualified key with that name whose target
matches the [=selected model=], in ascending code point order of the key. A target
matches when it equals, [=ASCII case-insensitively=], the model's family name,
its identifier or one of its aliases. The [=description=] given to the model is
formed from these values.

Qualified keys never select a model: `model` remains the ordered preference
list, like a font stack, and the core values are the fallback that every model
receives. Qualified keys whose target does not match the [=selected model=] are unused,
without a diagnostic.

Because refinements follow the core, a model that reads only the start of its
input, such as one with a 77-token text encoder, may never see them. Authors
who refine for such a model keep the core short, with the essentials first.

An [=image encoder=] typically
evolves the core first and then adds refinements for the models in its
preference list.

## Never document intent ## {#parameters-not-intent}

The following are settings of the user's renderer, never parameters of a
document, and renderers MUST NOT let any key, standard or vendor, set them: output format,
number of candidates, quality or speed tier, resolution beyond the layout
box, safety and moderation settings, provider endpoint, credentials, and
anything that affects billing. See [[#security-forwarding]].

# Rendering # {#rendering}

A renderer turns a document into an image. This section defines what every
renderer MUST do with the interpreted parameters and what it MUST report; how
it produces pixels is outside this specification.

<div class=note>

Renderer checklist (informative):

1. compute the interpreted parameters ([[#parameters-interpretation]]);
2. select the model ([[#model-matching]]);
3. resolve the mode and the input image ([[#rendering-modes]], [[#security-inputs]]);
4. assemble the model input ([[#rendering-prompt]]);
5. forward vendor keys only through the mapping ([[#security-forwarding]]);
6. size the output ([[#rendering-size]]);
7. report every interpreter and renderer code ([[#rendering-diagnostics]]);
8. handle failure ([[#rendering-failure]]).

</div>

## Modes and the original image ## {#rendering-modes}

The [=original image=] is the carrier's own image (an `<img>` element's
`currentSrc` at render time) or, when present, the image named by `source`.
`mode` states how the document relates to it; a viewer's settings always take
precedence over `mode` ([[#viewer-requirements]]).

: `generate` (default)
:: Render from the description alone. A viewer shows the original image, or
    the `preview`, until the render is ready, then replaces it, and falls back
    to the original on failure.
: `transform`
:: As `generate`, but the original image is an input to generation, governed
    by `strength`. Model matching considers only models that accept an image
    input; if none of the preferred models does, the renderer's default model
    is used when it accepts an image, and otherwise the document is rendered
    as `generate` with `mode-degraded`. If there is no original image and no
    readable `source` ([[#security-inputs]]), the document is rendered as
    `generate` with `original-not-readable`. As in `generate`, the
    description states what the resulting image shows, including what is
    kept from the original; it is not an edit instruction. It may be absent
    when only `style` applies. Because a `transform` document is rendered as
    `generate` when the original cannot be read, a description that names its
    subject still produces a fitting image.
: `keep`
:: The original image is the intended image; the description is information
    about it. A viewer MUST NOT render a `keep` document automatically, even
    under an automatic site setting; a render the user requests is a
    `generate` render unless the user asks for a transformation.

<div class=note>
A renderer whose model expects an instruction can wrap the description (for
example "Make this image show: …"); the text sent is reported as for any
render ([[#rendering-prompt]]).
</div>

## Assembling the model input ## {#rendering-prompt}

- A renderer MUST send the model the [=description=] it intends, explicitly,
    even when the document has no `prompt` (an empty description); a provider's
    default prompt is never a JAI default.
- `style` and `avoid` values MUST reach the model, through a native input or
    as prompt text, or be reported with `unsupported-parameter`. `avoid` values
    are sent as the model's negative prompt, joined with `, `, when it has one;
    when it has none the renderer MAY fold them into the prompt in wording the
    model is known to honour, reporting `folded-into-prompt`, or omit them,
    reporting `unsupported-parameter`. Silent omission is not conforming.
- A renderer MUST NOT fail a render because of the length of the
    description. A renderer that shortens its input reports `adjusted-value`
    for `prompt` and SHOULD drop text from the end of the description before
    dropping any `style` value, using chunked encoding where the model allows
    it.
- A renderer derives rendering parameters only from the document's members.
    Text inside values never changes a parameter, a renderer setting or a
    provider setting: `prompt` is content, never instructions, including for
    renderers that involve a language model.
- A renderer MUST be able to show the user the exact text it sent to the
    model.

<div class=note>
The reference renderer assembles the prompt values joined with line feeds,
then the style values as a comma-separated list, then, only when folding, a
final line listing what to avoid. On models with a short token window, style
terms belong early. Provider-side prompt rewriting is a user setting of the
renderer, not a document matter.
</div>

## Size and aspect ## {#rendering-size}

The <dfn export>governing ratio</dfn> of an element is the aspect ratio of its
[=original image=] if it has one, and otherwise the ratio of an author-sized
[=layout box=] (both `width` and `height` attributes, or a CSS `aspect-ratio`)
if there is one. A standalone document (a `.jai` file opened on its own) has no
governing ratio.

- When a governing ratio exists, the viewer renders at it, and the interpreted
    `aspect`, if present and different, is reported with `ignored-value`. A
    ratio differs when the two ratios differ by more than 1% (|ln(*a*/*g*)| >
    0.01); `landscape` differs from a governing ratio that is not greater than
    1, and `portrait` from one that is not less than 1.
- Otherwise the ratio is, in order: the interpreted `aspect`; the ratio of the
    `preview`; the renderer's default.

`aspect` therefore shapes renders of unsized elements and standalone files, and
records the author's intent everywhere. On a page, the shape the author gave
the element wins: authors who set both make them agree.

Renderers snap to the nearest ratio they support ([[#key-aspect]]). The layout
box always governs display: when the render's ratio still differs from the box
(for example after snapping), the viewer honours the page's `object-fit` and
`object-position`; if the page sets neither, it crops the render to cover the
box, centred. A viewer MUST NOT stretch a render to a box of a different ratio.

Viewers SHOULD request a render no smaller than the element's displayed CSS
size multiplied by the device pixel ratio, within the user's cost policy, and
never larger than the user's configured maximum.

## Renderer diagnostics ## {#rendering-diagnostics}

A renderer MUST NOT refuse to render a valid document because of the value of
any standard key, and MUST report, with every code of class `renderer` or
`interpreter` in [[#diagnostics]], every standard key it did not honour as
written. A viewer MUST make those reports available to the user.

## Failure ## {#rendering-failure}

A render fails when it produces no usable image: a provider or network error,
a refusal or content filter (including a blank or black frame), a timeout,
or a cancellation. On failure a viewer MUST restore the original image, or in
the absence of one the `preview` or the description, MUST NOT leave a
permanent placeholder, and MUST NOT retry with an altered description. A
refusal is a normal outcome: viewers MUST NOT rewrite the description, or let a
document cause it to be rewritten, to evade a refusal.

# Encoding images # {#image-encoding}

*This section is informative.*

An <dfn>image encoder</dfn> produces a document from an existing image. It is
a tool, not a conformance class: its output is an ordinary document, and the
[=encoder=] class above only concerns the file form. Nothing here constrains
how an image encoder works; the section describes the approach that the
compression view in [[#intro-compression]] suggests, and the conventions that
make encoded documents easy to recognise and reuse.

## Analysis by synthesis ## {#image-encoding-loop}

An image encoder searches for the description whose renders look most like
the original, using the target models themselves as the decoder:

1. **Describe.** A vision-capable model writes a first description of the
    original, following [[#authoring]]: medium first, subject and counts,
    composition, light and colour, literal text in quotes.
2. **Render.** Each candidate description is rendered with every target model,
    with fixed seeds so that differences between candidates come from the
    text, not from noise.
3. **Compare.** Each render is scored against the original. Useful measures
    are semantic (cosine similarity of image embeddings such as CLIP),
    perceptual (colour and layout distance between small thumbnails), and a
    vision model's list of the visible differences. The fitness of a
    candidate is its mean score across the target models.
4. **Revise.** The best candidates are kept, and new ones are written from
    them by addressing the listed differences, while keeping what already
    matches.
5. **Refine per model.** Once the core description stops improving, the
    encoder looks at models that lag behind and tries short
    [[#model-refinements|per-model refinements]] for them, keeping a refinement
    only when it raises that model's score.
6. **Stop.** On a budget, a time limit, or when scores reach a plateau, the
    encoder returns the best document found so far.

This is the loop a CELP speech coder runs, with a description in place of
codebook indices and a generative model in place of the synthesis filter. The
result is a code tuned to the decoders the author chose. Other models still
receive the core description, which remains a faithful general description of
the image.

The quality of an encoded document is bounded by its decoders: a model that
cannot draw hands, legible text or exact counts will not do so because the
description is better. Scores measure resemblance, not truth; an encoder that
optimises an embedding score alone can drift towards text that games the
metric, which is why the comparison step also uses a reader of the images.

## Output conventions ## {#image-encoding-output}

An image encoder is expected to produce a document that a person could have
written by hand, so that it stays portable and editable:

- `prompt` holds the core description, complete enough for any model;
    `prompt/<target>` (and, rarely, `style/<target>` or `avoid/<target>`) holds
    the per-model refinements, with the target set to the model's family name
    when only one target model is in that family, else to its alias or
    identifier.
- `model` lists the target models, best-scoring first, so that viewers that
    have several of them prefer the one that reproduced the image best.
- `aspect` is the ratio of the original's pixel dimensions, reduced to small
    whole numbers when that changes the ratio by less than 1%.
- `seed` is left out unless the author wants exact reproduction with one
    model: a description tuned on one seed is still meant to work on any.
- `preview`, when wanted, is a tiny placeholder derived from the original,
    within the bounds of [[#security-bounds]].
- Scores and the encoder's settings, when recorded at all, go in the tool's
    own vendor keys. Because vendor keys are part of the identity and add
    bytes, published documents usually leave them out and keep the report
    beside the document instead.
- The original image is not embedded. An encoded document describes the
    original; it is not a copy of it, and it does not carry the original's
    provenance or rights information ([[#security-provenance]]).

# HTML and URL integration # {#html}

## Carriers ## {#html-carriers}

A document reaches an `<img>` element through one of four [=carriers=]. A
viewer examines them in the order below and takes the first one found; the
same order is the recommended order for authors.

<dl>
<dt><dfn export>carrier 1</dfn>: `<picture>` with `<source type="image/jai">`
<dd>
```html
<picture>
  <source type="image/jai" srcset="castle.jai">
  <img src="castle.png" alt="A magical castle at dusk">
</picture>
```
This is HTML's own format negotiation: a browser that does not support
`image/jai` skips the `<source>` without fetching it and shows the raster
[[!HTML]]. `src` and `alt` stay untouched, the `.jai` is fetched only when a
viewer wants to render, and a browser that one day decodes `image/jai`
natively needs no markup change. The `srcset` may name a `.jai` resource or
hold a `data:image/jai` URL.

<dt><dfn export>carrier 2</dfn>: the `data-jai` attribute
<dd>
```html
<img src="castle.png" alt="A magical castle at dusk"
     data-jai="A magical castle at dusk;style=anime">
```
The attribute holds the text form; the `jai:` prefix is optional. It is never
read as the JSON form: a JSON object in `data-jai` is prose. This is the inline
form for markup that cannot be restructured.

<div class=note>
HTML intends `data-*` attributes for a page's own scripts and recommends type
negotiation for extensions shared across tools, which is why carrier 1 is
preferred. Carrier 2 is kept because it survives most sanitisers and needs
no extra element.
</div>

<dt><dfn export>carrier 3</dfn>: `src` is a `data:image/jai` URL
<dd>
```html
<img src="data:image/jai,A%20magical%20castle%20at%20dusk;style=anime"
     alt="A magical castle at dusk" width="800" height="600">
```
The pure form: the image *is* the description and there are no fallback
pixels. In every current browser this is an unavailable image whose `alt`
text is what the reader sees; see [[#html-carrier-3]].

<dt><dfn export>carrier 4</dfn>: `alt` beginning with `jai:` (deprecated)
<dd>
```html
<img src="castle.png" alt="jai:A magical castle at dusk;style=anime">
```
Deprecated. The accessible name of the image becomes the syntax, which
assistive technology announces, and every parameter is read aloud. Documents
SHOULD NOT use this carrier where any other is available; validators report
`alt-carrier`. It is recognised because it is the only carrier that Markdown
and strict sanitisers let through, and in those contexts a document should be
the prefix and a short description with few or no parameters. A document
MUST NOT place a `data:` URL (`preview`, `source` or any other value) in an
`alt` carrier (`alt-carrier-payload`). A viewer MUST NOT render automatically
from this carrier; it MAY offer rendering on explicit per-image request.
</dl>

Carriers 1 and 3 are HTML's own extension points and are the only carriers a
user agent that decodes `image/jai` natively implements. Carriers 2 and 4 are
defined for scripts and extensions acting for a user on a page that opted in;
a native user agent ignores them unless the user enables such a viewer.

An `alt` attribute that does not begin, after leading [=JAI whitespace=], with
`jai:` is never a JAI document. A `jai:` string in any URL slot (`src`,
`href`, `srcset`, CSS `url()`) is not a carrier: `jai:` is a text prefix and
this specification defines no `jai:` URL scheme (`jai-scheme-url`).

### Discovery ### {#html-discovery}

A viewer runs <dfn export>discovery</dfn> over the `<img>` elements of a document in
tree order, descending into open shadow roots and skipping closed ones, and
re-runs it on the mutations HTML considers relevant for source selection
(changes to `src`, `srcset`, `sizes`, `media`, `type`, and to the children of
a `<picture>`), as well as on `data-jai` and `alt` changes. Discovery MUST NOT
depend on the image having been fetched or having failed: lazily loaded
images fire no events until they approach the viewport.

For an `<img>` element:

1. If the element's parent is a `<picture>`: consider each `<source>` that
    precedes the `<img>` among the parent's children, in tree order, whose
    `type` attribute has the essence `image/jai` and whose `media` attribute
    is absent or matches the environment. Parse its `srcset` with HTML's
    *parse a srcset attribute* algorithm [[!HTML]]; a candidate whose
    descriptors are invalid is dropped exactly as HTML drops it. If no
    candidate remains, skip the `<source>` (validators report
    `srcset-unparseable`). Otherwise let *url* be the first candidate's URL;
    descriptors play no further part. If *url* is a `data:` URL and the data:
    URL processor [[!FETCH]] returns failure, or returns a MIME type whose
    essence is not `image/jai`, skip the `<source>`. Otherwise the document is
    the resource at *url*, resolved against the element's base URL, and no
    later `<source>` is examined. `width` and `height` on the `<source>` are
    layout hints. An implementation that cannot evaluate media queries treats
    every `media` value as matching.
2. Otherwise, for a script or extension viewer: if the element has a
    `data-jai` attribute, the document is its value parsed with the [=text
    parsing algorithm=].
3. Otherwise, if the element's `src` attribute, parsed against the base URL,
    is a `data:` URL that the data: URL processor accepts and whose MIME type
    essence is `image/jai`: the document is its body ([[#data-urls]]).
4. Otherwise, for a script or extension viewer: if the element's `alt`
    attribute begins, after JAI whitespace, with `jai:` ASCII
    case-insensitively, the document is the attribute value parsed with the
    text parsing algorithm (carrier 4).
5. Otherwise the element carries no document.

If the element's `currentSrc` is the URL selected in step 1 and the element
is complete with a natural width greater than zero, the user agent decodes
`image/jai` natively and the viewer stands down.

A `<source>` without a `type` attribute is selected by the browser and
yields a broken image; validators report `source-missing-type`. A `<source>`
after the `<img>` is ignored by HTML (`source-after-img`). A JAI URL MUST NOT
appear in the `<img>`'s own `srcset`. Authors place the `image/jai` source
before raster sources when JAI-capable user agents should prefer it; an
extension's discovery does not depend on the order. A `<source
type="image/jai">` for a document with `mode=keep` is unusual, since a native
decoder would then never show the raster (`keep-in-source`).

<div class=note>
A user agent that decodes `image/jai` natively would select the JAI source
and never load the raster original; `mode` then applies only with an
explicit `source`. HTML never re-selects a source after a decode fails, so
native support will need a rule that a declined or failed render re-selects
the next candidate; the editors intend to raise this with the HTML
Standard's maintainers when a native implementation exists.
</div>

### Requirements for carrier 3 ### {#html-carrier-3}

An `<img>` whose `src` is a `data:image/jai` URL:

- MUST have a non-empty `alt` that is prose (the description or a human
    summary); in every current browser the `alt` is what the reader gets;
- SHOULD have `width` and `height` attributes or a CSS `aspect-ratio`, since
    it has no intrinsic size until a render arrives;
- MUST NOT have a `srcset`;
- SHOULD NOT be the `<img>` of a `<picture>` that also carries a JAI
    `<source>`.

Authors are warned that pages without a JAI viewer show an unavailable
image; that `onerror` handlers and lazy-loading libraries rewrite a broken
image's `src` (viewers capture the carrier at discovery); that a Content
Security Policy must list `data:` in `img-src`; and that carrier 1 is
preferred whenever fallback pixels exist. The inline pure form with a
fallback is carrier 1 with a `data:` URL in `srcset`:

```html
<picture>
  <source type="image/jai" srcset="data:image/jai,A%20magical%20castle;style=anime">
  <img src="castle.png" alt="A magical castle">
</picture>
```

### Serving `.jai` resources ### {#html-serving}

*This section is informative.* See [[#media-type]] for the server
requirements. A script on another origin can read a `.jai` resource only when
it is served with CORS headers; browser extensions with host permissions
fetch it from their privileged context but are still bound by
[[#security-inputs]] for any *image* input. Appendix [[#server-config]] gives server mappings.

## Transport decoding ## {#html-transport}

Exactly one transport decoding is applied per carrier, before the
[=decoding algorithm=] or the [=text parsing algorithm=] runs:

- `data:` URL (carrier 1 with a `data:` URL in `srcset`, carrier 3): parse
    the attribute value against the document's base URL and run the data: URL
    processor of the Fetch Standard [[!FETCH]]. The fragment is excluded; the
    percent-decoding is the URL Standard's byte-level percent-decode [[!URL]],
    which leaves a `%` not followed by two hexadecimal digits in place and
    never fails. Decode the resulting bytes with [[#decoding]].
- http(s) URL (carrier 1 with a resource URL): the response body, decoded
    with [[#decoding]] regardless of the served `Content-Type`.
- `data-jai` and `alt` attributes (carriers 2 and 4): no transport decoding;
    the attribute value, as the HTML parser produced it, is the input.
- Query forms: [[#url-query]].

No second percent-decoding is ever applied. Implementations MUST NOT use
decoders that throw on malformed input (`decodeURIComponent`) in a transport
step. Transport decoding never changes JAI syntax: `%3B` and `%3D` become `;`
and `=` and parse as delimiters.

<div class=note>
Reading the `src` attribute and reading the element's `src` property give
the same `data:` body by construction, because the data: URL processor
starts from the parsed URL either way.
</div>

## Query forms ## {#url-query}

Two conventions carry a document in a URL's query, for playgrounds, gateways
and links in text channels.

: <dfn export>whole-query form</dfn>
:: `https://example.org/play?prompt=A%20magical%20castle;style=anime`

    Parse the URL with the URL parser [[!URL]]. If its query is null, the URL
    carries no document. Otherwise percent-decode the query once as in
    [[#html-transport]], decode the bytes with *UTF-8 decode without BOM*
    [[!ENCODING]] (ill-formed sequences become U+FFFD), and run the [=text
    parsing algorithm=] on the result; an empty query is the empty document.
    The JSON test of [[#decoding]] does not apply. `+` is a literal plus sign
    and `&` is ordinary text; the URL carries no other parameters.
    Generators MUST percent-encode space as `%20` (never `+`), `%`, `#` and
    ASCII whitespace, and MAY leave `;`, `=`, `+`, `&`, `:`, `/` and non-ASCII
    unencoded. Form serialisers and parsers (`URLSearchParams`, `<form
    method=get>`) MUST NOT be used to build or read this form.
: <dfn export>parameter form</dfn>
:: `https://example.org/render?jai=A%20magical%20castle%3Bstyle%3Danime&w=800`

    A query parameter named `jai`, in `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`
    [[!URL]], whose value is a text-form document. Parse the URL with the URL
    parser; if its query is null there is no document. Otherwise parse the
    query with the `application/x-www-form-urlencoded` parser; the value of
    the first pair named `jai` is run through the [=text parsing algorithm=]
    (an empty value is the empty document). With no such pair there is no
    document. Raw tabs and newlines have already been removed by the URL
    parser. It is read by any form parser, so `+` is a space and `&` separates parameters; generators encode
    `;`, `=`, `&`, `+`, `#`, `%` and whitespace in the value. This form
    coexists with other parameters and is the convention for a *gateway*: a
    server that renders a document to pixels so that `<img src="https://gateway/…?jai=…">`
    displays everywhere today. Hosting a gateway is outside this
    specification; a gateway that stores or publishes prompts or watermarks
    output must say so to its users. A JAI-aware viewer MAY, with the user's
    consent, decode the parameter locally instead of fetching the gateway.

## Other host formats ## {#html-other}

*This section is informative.*

- **Markdown.** CommonMark image syntax exposes only `src` and `alt`, so the
    only Markdown carrier is carrier 4 (`![jai:A magical castle](castle.png)`),
    or a gateway URL in the parameter form. Where a Markdown host allows raw
    HTML, carrier 1 is preferred. Bare spaces break Markdown destinations; use
    percent-encoding or angle brackets.
- **CSS.** `image-set(url("castle.jai") type("image/jai"), url("castle.png")
    type("image/png"))` is the CSS twin of carrier 1; this edition does not
    require viewers to act on CSS images or on SVG `<image>`.
- **`<object type="image/jai" data="castle.jai">`** renders its fallback
    content in current browsers but may download the resource and is governed
    by `object-src`; it is not a carrier.
- **Links.** A link to a `.jai` file behaves like a link to any file the
    browser cannot display. There is no navigable JAI link form in this
    edition.
- **Service workers.** A site can serve `<img src="castle.jai">` to its own
    visitors today by rendering in a service worker; this works over http(s)
    only and not on a first visit.
- **Clipboard.** The interoperable clipboard representation is `text/plain`
    containing the canonical text form with its `jai:` prefix.

### Carriers by context ### {#html-context-matrix}

*This section is informative.* Sanitisers differ, and the carrier an author
can use depends on the pipeline:

<table class=data>
<thead><tr><th>Context<th>Usable carriers
<tbody>
<tr><td>HTML the author controls<td>all; prefer carrier 1
<tr><td>User content through DOMPurify defaults<td>carriers 2 and 3, and carrier 1 with an http(s) or relative <code>.jai</code> URL (<code>data:</code> URLs in <code>srcset</code> are removed)
<tr><td>User content through GitHub-style allow-list sanitisers<td>carrier 1 as raw HTML (<code>&lt;picture&gt;</code>, <code>&lt;source&gt;</code>, <code>srcset</code>, <code>type</code> survive; <code>data-*</code> and <code>data:</code> <code>src</code> do not), else carrier 4
<tr><td>CommonMark image syntax<td>carrier 4 only, or a gateway URL
<tr><td>Chat, documentation, email text<td>the text form with its prefix
</table>

## Viewer requirements ## {#viewer-requirements}

A [=viewer=] decides whether, when and how documents are rendered. The user
always wins: user settings (never render, ask, render on request, render
automatically for a site) override `mode` and every other author preference,
except that `keep` is never rendered without a user request
([[#rendering-modes]]), and the consent, budget and disclosure requirements of
[[#security-consent]] apply to every render.

A viewer MUST NOT present a render as saving data or bandwidth when the
original image was fetched, and SHOULD NOT state any saving unless it avoided
fetching the original.

### Replacement ### {#viewer-replacement}

A viewer that replaces an image with a render:

1. MUST make the replacement effective under HTML's image source selection
    and MUST verify it (the `load` event and `currentSrc`). Setting `src`
    alone is a no-op when the element has a `srcset` or a preceding matching
    `<source>`. RECOMMENDED: inside a `<picture>`, insert a viewer-owned
    `<source>` whose `srcset` is the rendered image as the first child and
    remove it to restore; otherwise set the element's `srcset`, recording the
    previous value.
2. MUST keep the layout box: the element's `width` and `height` or CSS
    `aspect-ratio`, else the original's ratio.
3. MUST NOT alter `id`, `class`, event listeners, or link ancestors, and
    MUST keep the element in place where possible; a replacement element
    carries `role="img"` and the same accessible name and description.
4. MUST record every state it changes and restore all of it on request and
    on failure, and MUST let the user compare the original and the render.
5. MUST NOT replace the same element more than once per [=identity=] per page
    load, and MUST NOT re-render on resize or scroll. If the page changes
    `src`, `srcset` or the `<picture>` children of a replaced element, the
    viewer treats the element as new and releases its saved state.
6. SHOULD NOT, under an automatic setting, start rendering before the user
    agent would fetch the image (for `loading=lazy`, not before it is near the
    viewport), and MUST NOT render elements that are not displayed.
7. MUST obtain the rendered bytes itself and insert them from local data; it
    MUST NOT point a page element at a provider URL (which would leak the
    page through `Referer` and reveal the provider).
8. MUST confirm that a replacement loaded (`load`, natural width greater than
    zero) and MUST NOT retry the same URL. Depending on the engine and on how
    the viewer is implemented, replacement loads may be governed by the
    page's Content Security Policy [[!CSP3]], under which `'self'` never
    matches `blob:` and `*` never matches `data:`. (Chrome currently exempts
    loads initiated by an extension's content script from the page's policy;
    other engines and page scripts are not exempt.) When a replacement does
    not load, the viewer either restores the original or presents the render
    without a page-initiated fetch (for example an `ImageBitmap` painted into a
    canvas sized to the layout box with `role="img"` and the original
    accessible name) or in viewer-owned UI. Viewers SHOULD prefer a `data:` URL
    of the rendered bytes over a `blob:` URL, which more policies permit.
9. Viewer UI injected into a page is subject to the page's `style-src` and
    `script-src`; a shadow root with constructed stylesheets avoids both.

### Accessibility ### {#viewer-accessibility}

1. A viewer MUST keep a human-written `alt` unchanged, including `alt=""`. If
    the `alt` carried the document (carrier 4) or is absent, the accessible
    name becomes the first `prompt` value with control characters
    removed, truncated at a grapheme cluster boundary at about 250 code
    points, with the full text available in the viewer's own UI; when the
    document has no `prompt`, a localised generic name. Keys, URLs and `data:`
    payloads never appear in an accessible name.
2. A viewer SHOULD expose to assistive technology what it shows sighted users about
    the image's state: that it was rendered by an AI model from a
    description, that it may differ from the publisher's image, and that the
    original is available. `aria-description` (or `aria-describedby`
    referring to an element the viewer owns) carries this; `title` does not.
    `aria-busy` is set while a render is pending.
3. Per-image controls MUST be keyboard-operable and named, never hover-only.
4. Under `prefers-reduced-motion` the swap SHOULD happen without transitions.
    Individual replacements are not announced to assistive technology by
    default.
5. For `animate`: under `prefers-reduced-motion` a viewer shows a still
    frame until the user starts the animation; it shows the first frame until
    the user starts playback unless the user has opted into autoplay for the
    site; it provides a keyboard-operable control to pause, stop or hide
    animation longer than five seconds [[!WCAG22]]; renderers SHOULD instruct
    the model to avoid flashing; viewers MAY refuse to play animation they
    detect as flashing.
6. The viewer's own UI (consent, provider configuration, per-image controls,
    diagnostics) MUST be keyboard-operable and expose names, roles and states.
7. A JAI-aware user agent MAY, without rendering, expose the description as
    the original image's accessible description; it MUST NOT replace a
    human-written `alt` with it.

Generated imagery carries no authority: logos, text and interface elements
inside a render are model output. Viewers mark rendered images as such in
viewer-owned UI and always offer the original.

The page's own accessibility conformance is unaffected by what a user's agent
does at the user's request; a conforming viewer is a user agent in the sense
of the User Agent Accessibility Guidelines.

### Personalisation ### {#viewer-personalisation}

At the user's request, a viewer MAY render a document with transformations the
user chose: for example a preferred style, a sensitivity or accessibility
adjustment, the text inside the image translated into the user's language, or
a mood. A personalised render:

1. MUST be marked as personalised, in viewer-owned UI and to assistive
    technology, alongside the AI-rendered state ([[#viewer-accessibility]]);
2. MUST leave the document unchanged, and the viewer MUST keep a render
    without personalisation available to the user;
3. is reported like any render: the text sent to the model includes the
    personalisation ([[#rendering-prompt]]).

Personalisation is a user setting. A document or a page MUST NOT be able to
request or trigger it, and a viewer MUST NOT add a personalisation in order to
get past a refusal ([[#rendering-failure]]).

# Extensibility # {#extensibility}

- Keys without a `.` are reserved for this specification and are only
    assigned by a revision of it. Validators report unassigned undotted keys
    (`unknown-key`).
- Anyone may define keys of the form `vendor.name`, where `vendor` is a
    segment they control (`comfy.sampler`, `acme.lighting`). Serialisers
    preserve them; renderers that do not understand them ignore them; renderers
    that do understand them honour them only through the mapping rules of
    [[#security-forwarding]].
- The segment `dc` is reserved for Dublin Core terms: `dc.title`,
    `dc.creator`, `dc.description`, `dc.created`, `dc.language` (a BCP 47 tag
    for the language of the document's text), `dc.rights`, `dc.license`. A
    viewer that writes document text into a page MAY set `lang` from
    `dc.language`.
- Members of the JSON form whose value is an object, or an array containing
    non-strings, are reserved for future revisions ([[#json-shape]]). Future
    revisions will add keys and member shapes and will never remove a key or
    change the meaning of an existing one; a key may be deprecated with a
    diagnostic. Sequences, variants and multi-image documents, if ever defined,
    will use such members or a distinct media type, never additional gzip
    members.
- There is no registry: this specification is the registry of undotted
    keys, and each vendor owns its segment. Appendix [[#families]] and the
    Dublin Core list above are informative.

# Security and privacy considerations # {#security}

Decoding a JAI document has side effects that no other image format has: it
transmits the document, and possibly an input image, to a renderer; the
render is performed under the user's provider account and is attributable to
the user; and it usually costs money. Every requirement in this section
follows from that.

## Untrusted input and bounds ## {#security-bounds}

All JAI input is untrusted. Parsing, reading and decoding are total
([[#conformance-determinism]]): no input causes an exception, a crash, a
hang, or unbounded resource use.

- Decoders MUST bound the decompressed size of a gzip member, enforced during
    inflation, and the size of any text or JSON input that did not come from
    gzip. The bounds MUST be at least 16 MiB and 1 MiB respectively; those
    values are RECOMMENDED. Exceeding the decompression bound yields the empty
    document with `malformed-container`; exceeding the text bound yields the
    empty document with `undecodable`. Viewers MAY apply the text bound to
    attribute carriers.
- Viewers SHOULD decode a document only when the element is displayed and a
    render may follow, and SHOULD cap the length of values they transmit to a
    renderer, reporting `value-truncated`.
- Viewers SHOULD bound the dimensions of any image they decode as an input
    or preview (RECOMMENDED 16 megapixels) and MUST re-encode input images
    before transmission, which also strips their metadata.
- A viewer never interprets a value inside a document as another document,
    never runs discovery on elements it inserted or modified itself, and
    displays a `preview` only through an image decoder, never as markup.

## Permitted inputs ## {#security-inputs}

A viewer MUST NOT use as an input to rendering any image data that the
embedding document could not itself read. In a browser that is exactly the
data that would not taint a canvas: same-origin images, `data:` images and
`blob:` images of the page's origin, and cross-origin images loaded with CORS
that succeeded for the document's origin. Input pixels are obtained with the
document's own privileges (a content script's canvas, or a fetch applying
the document's CORS and credential rules), never from a privileged context
that carries the user's cookies. When the implicit original is not readable,
`transform` behaves as `generate` with `original-not-readable` unless a
readable `source` is present. Publishers who want `transform` on cross-origin
originals serve them with CORS headers and `crossorigin="anonymous"`.

A `source` URL is fetched only with the carrier document's privileges and
only after the consent the render requires. Renders that transmit an input
image SHOULD require a per-image confirmation that shows the image and names
its origin.

## Selectors, never locators ## {#security-selectors}

`model` is a selector: a viewer matches its values only against renderers
and models the user has configured and enabled. A viewer MUST NOT dispatch to
a model, package, endpoint or workflow because a document names it, even
inside a configured provider's namespace, and MUST NOT download, install,
load or execute any artefact in response to a `model` value or to any other
key, standard or vendor, that names weights, LoRAs, workflows, graphs or
packages. Unmatched values use the user's default renderer with
`no-matching-model` and are shown to the user as text, never as a link.
Viewers SHOULD let the user mark a renderer as available only on explicit
request.

## Parameter forwarding ## {#security-forwarding}

A document, through any standard or vendor key, MUST NOT be able to cause a
viewer or renderer to fetch a URL, load weights, workflows or code, select or alter the
endpoint, credentials, headers, webhooks or callbacks, change safety or
moderation settings, or exceed the user's configured cost policy (candidate
count, steps, resolution, duration).

A renderer MUST forward document parameters to a provider only through a
mapping it controls: standard keys through its own semantics, and vendor keys only
into the provider's model-input namespace, after validating each value
against the provider's published input schema and the user's limits, and only
when the key is not on the renderer's per-provider deny-list of inputs that
name artefacts, carry credentials, or affect safety, output count or billing.
A renderer MUST ignore a key it will not forward and report
`parameter-not-allowed`.
"Preserved when serialising" ([[#extensibility]]) binds serialisers, not
renderers.

<div class=note>
For a hosted Flux-family model the deny-list includes at least the inputs
that disable the safety checker, set the number of outputs, name LoRA or
other weight files, or carry third-party API tokens.
</div>

## Consent, budgets and cancellation ## {#security-consent}

- A render is requested by the user's agent under the user's provider
    account. Viewers MUST make this visible.
- A viewer MUST NOT render automatically on a top-level site the user has not
    enabled; the default for every site is "ask" or "on request". Third-party
    frames are enabled separately. "Always render" is an explicit per-site
    setting with a spending or concurrency bound.
- Automatic rendering MUST be bounded per page and per time window and run
    with bounded concurrency; limits are visible to the user and reaching one
    stops rendering. Elements not displayed to the user MUST NOT be rendered.
- Consent and per-image actions are taken in viewer-owned UI from trusted
    user events and show the site, the full `prompt`, `avoid` and `style` text,
    any input image and its origin, and the configured renderer that matched.
- The user MUST be able to cancel a render; a viewer SHOULD cancel the
    provider job and set provider-side time limits where the API offers them,
    and cancels on navigation.
- A viewer MUST NOT render automatically from carrier 4 or from a document
    with `unsupported-version`.
- A viewer SHOULD keep a local, clearable, unsynchronised record of renders:
    it is a browsing history.
- A viewer SHOULD NOT render automatically in private browsing and MUST NOT
    persist records from it.

## What leaves the browser ## {#security-disclosure}

A viewer sends a provider only the parameters its renderer maps and the input
image bytes it decoded and re-encoded locally. It MUST NOT send the page URL,
title, referrer, cookies, identity or other page content, and SHOULD send
image bytes rather than URLs. The viewer's disclosure names the provider and
links its retention terms. Any in-page UI, replacement or `.jai` fetch
reveals to the page that the user runs a viewer.

Credentials of the reference viewer are stored extension-locally, used only
from its background context, never exposed to pages or content scripts,
requested only in viewer UI, sent only to the configured endpoint, and
redacted from logs; the background context accepts a provider endpoint,
allow-listed parameters and image bytes, never arbitrary URLs.

## Caches ## {#security-cache}

A render cache keyed by [=identity=] is a cross-site history oracle. A cached
render is subject to the same per-site consent and the same user action as an
uncached one, and viewers SHOULD prevent documents from distinguishing
cross-site cache hits, by keying the cache on (identity, renderer
configuration, top-level site) or by applying comparable delay.

## Viewer UI hygiene ## {#security-ui}

Viewer UI MUST render every document value as text, never as markup; SHOULD
isolate bidirectional text (`unicode-bidi: plaintext` or `<bdi>`); MUST NOT
silently truncate (`prompt`, `avoid`, `style` and `model` are fully
available); SHOULD visualise or warn on control and invisible formatting
characters; MUST show the top-level site; and MUST display the *configured*
renderer that matched, never the document's `model` string, as the thing that
will run. The same rules
apply to playgrounds and validators, which are cross-site scripting surfaces
for `preview` values and generated HTML snippets.

## Guidance for publishers ## {#security-publishers}

Sites that accept user markup and do not intend to publish JAI SHOULD strip
`data-jai`, `alt` values beginning with `jai:` in any case, `data:image/jai`
URLs in `src` and `srcset`, and `<source type="image/jai">`. DOMPurify's
defaults keep all of them; strict allow-list sanitisers remove all but `alt`.
A `.jai` referenced by URL is fetched per viewer at render time and may change
after review; publishers who need stability inline the document or record its
identity. Injection through templated prose ([[#text-escaping]]) can truncate
or override a document but, because of the rest of this section, can neither
select a renderer nor spend beyond the user's limits.

## Provenance ## {#security-provenance}

Renderers that write files SHOULD set the IPTC `digitalSourceType` to
`trainedAlgorithmicMedia` (`compositeWithTrainedAlgorithmicMedia` for
`transform`) [[IPTC-PHOTOMETADATA]] and MUST NOT strip provider-applied
provenance such as C2PA manifests [[C2PA]] or watermarks when re-encoding.
Viewers mark rendered images as generated ([[#viewer-accessibility]]).

# Diagnostics # {#diagnostics}

A <dfn export>diagnostic</dfn> is a code from the table below, optionally with the
key it concerns, and with any human-readable message the implementation
chooses. Codes are stable identifiers; messages are not.

- *Parser* codes are emitted by parsers, JSON readers and decoders. They are
    normative: a conforming implementation emits exactly these codes for the
    conditions described, and the conformance fixtures assert them.
- *Validator* codes are advisory. A validator SHOULD emit them for the
    conditions described and MAY define additional codes.
- *Interpreter* codes are emitted by interpreters, including model matching.
    They are normative: for the conditions described, a conforming interpreter
    emits exactly these codes, and the `interpret` fixtures compare the
    interpreter codes it emits as an exact multiset, ignoring any other codes.
    An interpreter that also validates MAY emit validator codes alongside them.
- *Renderer* codes are emitted by renderers; a renderer MUST emit them when
    the condition occurs ([[#rendering-diagnostics]]).
- *Viewer* codes are emitted by viewers.

The Key column names the key a diagnostic concerns. When it names one, an
implementation MUST attach it to the diagnostic. Fixtures write a diagnostic as
`code` or `code:key`; a fixture that writes `code` alone does not constrain the
key.

<table class=codes>
<thead><tr><th>Code<th>Class<th>Key<th>Condition
<tbody>
<tr><td>`empty-segment`<td>parser<td>none<td>An interior empty or whitespace-only segment was ignored.
<tr><td>`empty-value`<td>parser<td>the key of the assignment or member (ASCII-lowercased; `prompt` for prose)<td>A value was empty after normalisation and was dropped.
<tr><td>`reserved-key`<td>parser<td>`jai`<td>An assignment to the reserved key `jai` was dropped.
<tr><td>`invalid-key`<td>parser<td>none (MAY carry the member name as written)<td>A JSON member name is not a valid key; the member was dropped.
<tr><td>`missing-marker`<td>parser<td>none<td>JSON object without a `jai` member, read in JAI context.
<tr><td>`unsupported-version`<td>parser<td>none<td>The `jai` member is an integer other than 1.
<tr><td>`invalid-marker`<td>parser<td>none<td>The `jai` member is not an integer.
<tr><td>`number-value`<td>parser<td>the key of the assignment or member (ASCII-lowercased; `prompt` for prose)<td>A JSON number or boolean was converted to a string.
<tr><td>`duplicate-member`<td>parser<td>`jai`<td>A member name other than `jai` lowercases to `jai`; the member was dropped. (Validators MAY also report an exact repetition of a member name with this code.)
<tr><td>`duplicate-key`<td>parser<td>the key of the assignment or member (ASCII-lowercased; `prompt` for prose)<td>Two JSON member names collided after ASCII lowercasing; values were concatenated.
<tr><td>`unsupported-member`<td>parser<td>the key of the assignment or member (ASCII-lowercased; `prompt` for prose)<td>A JSON object or nested array was dropped (reserved for future revisions).
<tr><td>`invalid-json`<td>parser<td>none<td>The JSON form could not be parsed; the document is empty.
<tr><td>`malformed-container`<td>parser<td>none<td>The gzip member was malformed, followed by bytes, over the bound, or did not contain JSON; the document is empty.
<tr><td>`undecodable`<td>parser<td>none<td>The bytes are not well-formed UTF-8 or exceed the text bound; the document is empty.
<tr><td>`first-segment-assignment`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>The first segment was an assignment to an unknown key and the document has no prompt.
<tr><td>`invalid-qualifier`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A [=qualified key=] whose name is not `prompt`, `style` or `avoid`.
<tr><td>`unknown-key`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>An undotted key that this specification does not define.
<tr><td>`continuation-into-single-valued-key`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>Prose continued the value of `aspect`, `mode`, `strength`, `seed` or `animate` (not `source` or `preview`, whose `data:` URLs contain semicolons; see `split-data-uri`).
<tr><td>`no-prompt`<td>validator<td>none<td>No prompt, and the interpreted `mode` is `generate`.
<tr><td>`bare-key`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A prose segment is exactly a standard key; `key=true` was probably intended.
<tr><td>`newline-assignment`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A value contains a line break followed by `key=`.
<tr><td>`prefix-in-prompt`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A prompt value still begins with `jai:`.
<tr><td>`stray-wrapper`<td>validator<td>none<td>The whole document is wrapped in quotes, brackets or a Markdown fence.
<tr><td>`split-data-uri`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>An unknown key such as `charset` or `name` follows a `source` or `preview` value: an unescaped `;` inside a data URL.
<tr><td>`url-split`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A URL-like value was followed by an unknown-key assignment.
<tr><td>`confusable-delimiter`<td>validator<td>none<td>A full-width or Arabic semicolon is followed by what looks like `key=`.
<tr><td>`confusable-equals`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A full-width equals sign follows a key.
<tr><td>`foreign-syntax`<td>validator<td>none<td>The text looks like another prompt dialect (`--ar 16:9`, `Negative prompt:`, `<lora:…>`).
<tr><td>`colon-assignment`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>`key: value` was written where `key=value` was probably intended.
<tr><td>`quoted-value`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A value was wrapped in quotation marks.
<tr><td>`percent-encoded-text`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A non-URL value contains `%XX` sequences and no `%25`.
<tr><td>`control-character`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A value contains a control character other than TAB and LF.
<tr><td>`invisible-char`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A value contains U+200B to U+200D, U+2060 or a non-initial U+FEFF.
<tr><td>`bidi-control`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A value contains U+202A to U+202E or U+2066 to U+2069.
<tr><td>`nonstandard-value`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A valid value in a non-canonical spelling (`16x9`); the canonical spelling is offered.
<tr><td>`invalid-value`<td>validator, interpreter, renderer<td>the key concerned<td>A standard key's value does not match its grammar and was ignored.
<tr><td>`negated-avoid`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>An `avoid` value begins with a negation.
<tr><td>`strength-without-transform`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>`strength` without `mode=transform`.
<tr><td>`repeated-single`<td>validator, interpreter, renderer<td>the key concerned<td>A single-valued key had several values; the last was used.
<tr><td>`model-looks-like-locator`<td>validator<td>the key concerned<td>A `model` value looks like a URL or a path.
<tr><td>`large-payload`<td>validator<td>the key concerned (none for the document-size condition)<td>A value exceeds 8 KiB or the document exceeds 64 KiB.
<tr><td>`non-canonical-encoding`<td>validator<td>none<td>A `.jai` file, or an `image/jai` resource fetched by URL (not a `data:` URL), is not in the file form.
<tr><td>`unencoded-url-text`<td>validator<td>none<td>A URL-slot value contains whitespace, `#` or a bare `%`.
<tr><td>`srcset-unparseable`<td>validator<td>none<td>The `srcset` of a `<source type="image/jai">` yields no candidate under HTML's srcset parsing (for example because of an unencoded space).
<tr><td>`jai-scheme-url`<td>validator<td>none<td>A `jai:` string in a URL slot.
<tr><td>`alt-carrier`<td>validator<td>none<td>Carrier 4 is used.
<tr><td>`alt-carrier-payload`<td>validator<td>none<td>A `data:` URL inside an `alt` carrier.
<tr><td>`missing-size`<td>validator<td>none<td>A carrier-3 image without `width`/`height` or `aspect-ratio`.
<tr><td>`attribute-split`<td>validator<td>none<td>An element carrying a JAI carrier has an attribute whose name contains `"`, `'`, `;` or `=`: an unescaped quote probably ended the carrier attribute early.
<tr><td>`data-url-missing-comma`<td>validator<td>none<td>A `data:image/jai` URL in a carrier has no `,`.
<tr><td>`data-url-parameter`<td>validator<td>none<td>A `data:image/jai` URL has a media-type parameter other than `base64` or `charset`.
<tr><td>`source-missing-type`<td>validator<td>none<td>A `<source>` naming a `.jai` or `data:image/jai` URL has no `type`.
<tr><td>`source-after-img`<td>validator<td>none<td>A JAI `<source>` follows the `<img>`.
<tr><td>`keep-in-source`<td>validator<td>none<td>A `<source type="image/jai">` carries a `mode=keep` document.
<tr><td>`no-matching-model`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>`model`<td>No `model` value selected a model (or the document has none); the default was used.
<tr><td>`substituted-model`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>`model`<td>A family match selected a model the author did not name exactly.
<tr><td>`version-ignored`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>`model`<td>A version suffix of a `model` value was ignored.
<tr><td>`mode-degraded`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>`mode`<td>`transform` with an input image was rendered as `generate` because the selected model accepts no image.
<tr><td>`original-not-readable`<td>renderer<td>`mode`<td>`transform` had no readable input image. Decided when inputs are fetched; interpreters do not emit it.
<tr><td>`unsupported-parameter`<td>renderer<td>the standard key concerned<td>A standard key could not be honoured by the model and was omitted.
<tr><td>`folded-into-prompt`<td>renderer<td>`avoid`<td>`avoid` values were folded into the prompt text.
<tr><td>`adjusted-value`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>the standard key concerned<td>A value was clamped, snapped, reduced or shortened.
<tr><td>`ignored-value`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>the standard key concerned<td>A value had no effect in this context (`aspect` with an original, `source` outside `transform`).
<tr><td>`seed-nondeterministic`<td>renderer<td>`seed`<td>The seed was passed but the mode is not deterministic.
<tr><td>`parameter-not-allowed`<td>renderer<td>the vendor key refused<td>A vendor key was refused by the forwarding rules.
<tr><td>`value-truncated`<td>viewer<td>the standard key concerned<td>A value was shortened before transmission.
<tr><td>`unsupported-preview`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>`preview`<td>A `preview` value is not an allowed raster `data:` URL.
<tr><td>`invalid-image`<td>interpreter, renderer<td>`source`<td>A `source` value is not an allowed raster image.
</table>

## Conformance fixtures ## {#fixtures}

Fixtures live under `tests/<class>/<class>.json` in the specification
repository. Each file is a JSON object with `class`, `edition` and `cases`.

Every case has `id` (unique across the suite), `since` and `rationale`. A
document is an object mapping each key to an array of strings. A diagnostic is
written `code` or `code:key` ([[#diagnostics]]). `diagnostics` is compared as
an exact multiset. `advisories` lists validator codes that an implementation
with a validator MUST emit; other codes are allowed. `no_advisories` lists
validator codes that it MUST NOT emit.

: `parse`
:: `input` (string) → `document`, `diagnostics`; optional `advisories`,
    `no_advisories`.
: `serialize`
:: `document` → `text` (canonical text), `json` (canonical JSON), `identity`
    (lowercase hex SHA-256); optional `bare` (bare serialisation) and `url`
    (canonical URL form, [[#data-urls]]); each field is compared when present.
: `roundtrip`
:: `document` → the document survives, and serialisation is a fixed point of,
    each of: the canonical text parsed with the [=text parsing algorithm=];
    the bare serialisation and the canonical URL form decoded with
    [[#decoding]] in a [=JAI context=]; the canonical JSON read with the
    [=JSON reading algorithm=]; the file form decoded with [[#decoding]].
: `json`
:: `input` (JSON text) and `context` (boolean, default `true`: whether the
    text is in a [=JAI context=]) → `document`, `diagnostics`.
: `decode`
:: `bytes` (base64) and optional `bound` (the decompression bound in bytes,
    default 16 MiB) → `document`, `diagnostics`; optional `advisories`,
    `no_advisories`.
: `url`
:: `url` (string) and `kind` → `document` and `diagnostics`, or
    `discovered: false` when the URL carries no document. `kind` `data`
    applies [[#data-urls]]; `query` and `param` apply the two forms of
    [[#url-query]].
: `html`
:: `html` (a fragment parsed as HTML's fragment parsing algorithm does, with
    declarative shadow roots attached; base URL `https://example.test/page/`;
    every `media` value matches) → `discovered`, one entry per `<img>` that
    carries a document, in the order of [[#html-discovery]]: `carrier` (1 to
    4), and either `document` and `diagnostics`, or `url`, the candidate URL as
    written in `srcset`, compared after resolving it against the base URL.
    Optional `advisories` and `no_advisories` apply to all carriers in the
    fragment.
: `interpret`
:: `document`; optional `catalog`, the configured models in configuration
    order, each `{id, aliases, family, image_input, default}` (`family` may be
    null; `default: true` marks the user's default model, otherwise the first
    entry is the default); optional `families`, the renderer's family list,
    mapping each family name to its spellings in order; optional
    `has_original` (boolean, default `false`: whether the carrier offers an
    original image); optional `governing_ratio` (number: the element's
    [=governing ratio=], absent for none); optional `selected` (the `id` of
    a catalog entry the user chose, which is then the selected model instead
    of the result of [[#model-matching]]) → `interpreted` and `diagnostics`. `interpreted` is
    compared only for the members present: `description` (string, after
    [[#model-refinements]]); `style` and `avoid` (arrays of strings, after
    [[#model-refinements]]); `aspect`
    (W/H as a number, `square` as 1, or the string `landscape` or
    `portrait`); `mode` (string, the interpreted value of the `mode` key);
    `strength` (number); `seed`, `source`, `preview` (string); `animate`
    (boolean); `model` (the `id` of the selected catalog entry); `ratio` (the ratio to render at under [[#rendering-size]], W/H
    as a number; `null` when the `preview` or the renderer's default decides). `null` means absent or invalid. `diagnostics` is the exact
    multiset of interpreter codes.

Fixtures are append-only from the first tagged edition: a changed expectation
is a new case id with an erratum, never a silent edit.

# Writing JAI by hand # {#authoring}

*This section is informative.*

1. Write the description first, as you would describe the picture to someone
    on the phone: what is in it, how it is arranged, the light, the colours,
    the medium. Ordinary punctuation, including semicolons, is fine.
2. Add settings after a semicolon, each as `name=value`: `;style=anime`,
    `;aspect=16:9`, `;avoid=text`. Repeat `style` and `avoid` for several
    items rather than listing them in one value.
3. Put `jai:` in front when the place you are writing in does not already say
    it is JAI (a chat message, a document). On a web page, put the text in a
    `data-jai` attribute (no prefix needed) or use the `<picture>` form; avoid
    `alt`, which screen readers read aloud.
4. If your description itself starts with a word followed by `=`, start with
    `prompt=` instead. If you need a literal `;` right before something that
    looks like `name=`, write `\;`.
5. For an existing photo you want restyled, add `;mode=transform` and
    `;strength=0.6`. Describe the finished picture, including what stays the
    same, not the change.
6. Name a model only if you care: `;model=flux`. It is a preference, not a
    command; the reader's own setup decides.
7. Check it in the playground: it shows what a reader's software will see and
    offers the canonical spelling to copy.

# Examples # {#examples}

*This section is informative.*

<div class=example id=ex-minimal>
A minimal description, equally valid with or without the prefix:
```
A red bicycle leaning against a whitewashed wall, afternoon sun
```
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-styles>
One description, three styles:
```
jai:A lighthouse on a cliff in a storm;style=watercolor
jai:A lighthouse on a cliff in a storm;style=anime
jai:A lighthouse on a cliff in a storm;style=35mm film photo;style=grainy
```
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-models>
Model preferences, in order, advisory:
```
jai:A still life of pears on pewter, Dutch golden age;model=flux;model=sdxl;aspect=4:3
```
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-transform>
Image-conditioned rendering, with the page's own image as the input:
```html
<picture>
  <source type="image/jai" srcset="data:image/jai,Fishing%20boats%20in%20a%20harbour%20at%20dawn,%20as%20a%20loose%20pencil%20sketch;mode=transform;strength=0.7">
  <img src="harbour.jpg" alt="Fishing boats in a harbour at dawn" crossorigin="anonymous" width="1200" height="800">
</picture>
```
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-keep>
A publisher's photograph with a description for agents and future readers,
not to be replaced automatically:
```html
<img src="team.jpg" alt="The four founders in front of the office"
     data-jai="Four people in casual clothes standing in front of a brick office building, overcast daylight;mode=keep">
```
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-file>
A `.jai` file's content, decompressed:
```json
{"jai":1,"prompt":["A magical castle on a hill at dusk, lanterns in the windows","Mist in the valley below"],"aspect":"16:9","avoid":["people","text"],"model":"flux","style":"storybook illustration"}
```
</div>

<div class=example id=ex-vendor>
A vendor extension and a Dublin Core title, preserved by every serialiser:
```
jai:A brass astrolabe on velvet;comfy.sampler=euler;dc.title=Astrolabe study
```
</div>

<h2 class=no-num id=iana>Appendix A. Media type registration template</h2>

*This appendix is informative until registration is pursued.* It follows the
form of RFC 6838 §5.6 [[RFC6838]].

: Type name
:: image
: Subtype name
:: jai
: Required parameters
:: none
: Optional parameters
:: none (`charset` has no meaning; content is always UTF-8)
: Encoding considerations
:: binary. The file form is a gzip member; the text and JSON forms are UTF-8.
: Security considerations
:: See [[#security]]. Decoding transmits content to a renderer and is
    performed under the viewer's account; decoders bound decompression;
    model references are selectors and are never fetched; documents are
    untrusted input and parsing is total.
: Interoperability considerations
:: Three serialisations are distinguished by their leading bytes
    ([[#decoding]]). Renderers produce different pixels for the same
    document by design.
: Published specification
:: This document.
: Applications that use this media type
:: Browsers and browser extensions, image generation tools, authoring tools,
    AI agents.
: Fragment identifier considerations
:: none
: Additional information
:: Deprecated alias: none. Magic numbers: `1F 8B` (gzip), then `{"jai":1`
    followed by `,` or `}` after decompression; or `{` for the uncompressed JSON form. File
    extension: `.jai`. Macintosh file type code: none.
